window.FCX_UPDATES = {"generated":"2026-11-28","featured":["it-starts-with-a-paper-file","the-marketplace-opens-extensions-arrive","two-years-of-focuscasex-the-long-way-to-simple"],"posts":[{"id":"two-years-of-focuscasex-the-long-way-to-simple","date":"2026-11-20","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"Two years of FocusCaseX: the long way to simple","excerpt":"Two years ago FocusCaseX began as a better page than paper, in clinics that kept records by hand. Tonight we look back at the whole road, and say thank you.","tags":["anniversary","focuscasex","optometry"],"body":"Two years ago tonight, the first version of FocusCaseX ran its first day in a clinic that had worked from paper for years. The patient records were physical files in a rack, the recall system was a diary, and the optometrist who let us in asked one question: will this survive a Monday.\n\nThe workspace came first: plain clinical pages you could read and print, built to survive a day without a network. We got that wrong several times before we got it small enough to be right, and the clinics that stayed with us through those Mondays taught us most of what the product still is.\n\nThen the product grew hands. The marketplace arrived, and with it the pack family: Extensions, themes, capsules, WorkBenches, all of them files you can carry. Android followed, so a WorkBench lands on the exam-room phone with a tap. Indian clinics took those files to screening camps and made them earn their keep.\n\nAnd this year, the terminal. The Console turned bare fcx into a front door, with menus that answer the keyboard and care that lives where the machine lives. We built it because the machine under the front desk deserved better than a cheat sheet taped to the wall.\n\nNone of it happened in a straight line, and none of it happened without the clinics that told us the truth: about the prints that came out wrong, the recalls that went stale, the camp mornings that tested everything at once. You could have been polite. You were useful instead, which is better.\n\nTwo years in, we are a small team making an offline-first EMR workspace for eye care, still priced in rupees, still shaped by every note a clinic sends us. The long way to simple turns out to be the only way. Happy second birthday, FocusCaseX, and thank you to every clinic that carried a file for us."},{"id":"focuscasex-2-9-sharper-everywhere","date":"2026-11-12","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 2.9: sharper everywhere","excerpt":"2.9 closes the second year: sharper boards, steadier pack installs, and the Console in the best shape it has ever been in.","tags":["release","console","packs"],"body":"Version numbers do not mean much until you say what changed for the person at the counter. So here is 2.9 in counter terms: the boards read sharper, the packs land steadier, and the Console, after a season of notes from clinics, is in the best shape we have shipped.\n\nSharper boards first. Text, tables and the live views optometrists stare at all day render crisper, especially on the mixed screens clinics actually own, from the old desktop monitor to the Android tablet that goes to camps. Sharpening there is not decoration; it is kinder to eyes that have already worked eight hours.\n\nSteadier packs next. The .fcx family installs with fewer interruptions, reports its progress plainly, and recovers from a bad link without leaving half a theme behind. Installs also say what they are doing as they go, which sounds minor until the front desk is watching a progress bar instead of guessing.\n\nAnd the Console. CLI 3.0 shipped five weeks ago and clinics have been writing since: menu order, label wording, the occasional wrong turn. 2.9 carries that feedback in, and the Console rides along in its best shape. Bare fcx, same menus, fewer rough edges.\n\nThis is the version the second year closes on. Update when the day allows, walk the boards once, run fcx and look around. Then tell us what the next one should be, because we are already keeping the list, and the list is the best part of this work."},{"id":"the-docs-rewritten-from-a-to-z","date":"2026-11-04","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"The docs, rewritten from A to Z","excerpt":"The full guide is live: every board, every command demonstrated, every pack format, all on one page, with a contents that follows you as you read.","tags":["docs","guide","optometry"],"body":"Documentation is where software tells the truth about itself. For a while ours told the truth in pieces, spread across pages that aged at different speeds, and clinics noticed before we did. Today the full guide lands, rewritten from A to Z, and for the first time we feel the docs say what the product actually is: an offline-first workspace for eye care, with a terminal attached.\n\nEverything is on one page. Every board in the workspace, every pack format from .fcxext to .fcxwb, the authoring guides for Extensions and themes, the licence and trusted devices, the Console and its menus, the agents with their scopes and approvals. One page, one scroll, no treasure hunt.\n\nEvery command is demonstrated live, which we care about more than any prose. If the guide says a flag sorts recalls by due date, the guide shows it sorting. The same goes for the pack formats: each one shown being opened and installed, not described from a respectful distance. Clinics do not read about terminals, they run them, so the docs run alongside.\n\nThe contents follows you down the page. It marks where you are, and when you jump to a section at midnight before a camp, it keeps up. Small feature, kind intention.\n\nStart anywhere. New clinics will find their first evening in here; old hands will find the corners they never got around to. And when you spot a sentence that has drifted from the truth, tell us, because a guide rewritten A to Z still has to be kept honest one line at a time."},{"id":"six-months-of-packs-one-file-at-a-time","date":"2026-10-28","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Six months of packs, one file at a time","excerpt":"Half a year since the .fcx family shipped: clinics carry boards, themes and Extensions as files, and the marketplace has grown into a proper shelf.","tags":["packs","marketplace","extensions"],"body":"Six months ago the pack family shipped: .fcxext for Extensions, .fcxtheme for themes, .fcxcapsule for capsules, .fcxwb for WorkBenches. The idea was almost embarrassingly simple. A pack is a file. You open the file, the clinic gains the thing, done. No installer wizard, no steps to lose count of.\n\nHalf a year later, we have watched what clinics actually carry. WorkBenches travel the most: onto the Android phone in the exam room, between the two rooms that share an optometrist, out to screening camps where the internet is a rumour. Themes travel between clinics that share an owner. Capsules quietly move whole setups when a machine is renewed.\n\nThe marketplace has grown into a proper shelf in that time, past ninety items, and the shape of what clinics pick has surprised us a little: fewer decorative pieces than we guessed, more working tools, the Extensions that shave a step off a Tuesday. Clinics vote with their counters, not their curiosity.\n\nWhat we have learned is that the file format is the feature. One-click install from the file itself, on desktop and Android, means a pack can travel by whatever travels: a shared drive when it works, a cable when it does not, a phone passed across a desk. Nothing in the middle to negotiate with.\n\nWhere next? The authoring guides land soon, the marketplace keeps growing, and the Console now browses the shelf straight from the terminal. But the direction does not change: if a clinic can carry it as a file, it should stay that way. Six months in, we are more convinced of that than on the day .fcxext first opened."},{"id":"ten-minutes-that-save-an-hour-every-week","date":"2026-10-14","type":"blog","category":"Workflow Tips","title":"Ten minutes that save an hour every week","excerpt":"A Friday routine for clinic owners: export the week, sweep the recalls, sync the catalog, print one report. Ten minutes that keep Monday honest.","tags":["workflow","tips","optometry"],"body":"Friday evening, last patient gone, chairs still warm. Nobody wants to open software then, which is exactly why we are proposing a ten-minute routine that runs in the Console and pays for the whole weekend's peace of mind.\n\nFirst, export. One export of the week's patient files, off the machine, into the folder you actually back up. Offline-first does not mean backup-never, and the clinic that exports on a schedule is the clinic that shrugs at a failed disk.\n\nSecond, the recall sweep. Open care in the Console, look at the recall list, and close the week's loop: who was reminded, who came, who needs a different channel next time. Five minutes here is the difference between a recall system and a recall intention.\n\nThird, the catalog sync. Frame stock, lens prices, the items that quietly drift out of date. Sync it once a week and the bill you print on Monday agrees with the shelf you pulled it from.\n\nLast, one report. The single page you actually read, not the twelve you could. Print it, glance at it, put it where you will see it on Monday morning. If you do not know which page that is, ask whoever fixes your problems, because they always know.\n\nThat is the routine: export, sweep, sync, report. It fits in the time it takes the chai to cool, it runs happily from the terminal under the front desk, and it hands next week a starting point instead of a pile of maybes. We have started keeping Fridays for it ourselves, and we are not going back."},{"id":"fcx-cli-3-0-the-console-ships","date":"2026-10-06","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FCX CLI 3.0: the Console ships","excerpt":"FCX CLI 3.0 ships today: bare fcx opens the full-screen Console, with digit-key menus, care and diagnostics, and the marketplace browsed from the terminal.","tags":["cli","console","release"],"body":"Seven weeks ago we showed you a preview and asked what felt wrong. Clinics answered with notes, screenshots and a few strongly worded opinions about menu order. Today FCX CLI 3.0 ships, and bare fcx opens the Console, full-screen, the same on every machine from the front desk to the camp laptop.\n\nThe home screen lays out the clinic in plain menus: setup, account, Extensions, WorkBenches and care. No flags to memorise, no documentation open in another window. You read the screen and choose, the way you would read a noticeboard. It is the clinic's own map, drawn at eye level.\n\nThe keyboard carries you. Arrow keys move one line at a time, or press 1-9 to jump straight to the entry you already knew you wanted. Esc steps back a level, q quits. On the tired keyboards all of us have inherited, that difference adds up by mid-morning.\n\nCare and diagnostics live in the Console, so checking an installation and fixing it happen on the same screen. The marketplace browses from the terminal too: Extensions and themes described in place, installed when you decide. For clinics on slow links, browsing before downloading matters more than any feature list.\n\nWe keep saying the terminal is where clinics already live, and CLI 3.0 is us finally furnishing the room. Update, type fcx, and let the machine under the front desk show you around. Then tell us what to rearrange, because a home screen earns its name only after people have lived in it a while."},{"id":"authoring-teach-the-clinic-new-tricks","date":"2026-09-21","type":"blog","category":"Extensions","title":"Authoring: teach the clinic new tricks","excerpt":"Your first Extension is a manifest and one file of code, packed as .fcxext and installed with a tap. The authoring guides are on their way.","tags":["extensions","authoring","marketplace"],"body":"Every clinic we visit has one small thing they wish the software did. Not a new module, not a company-wide overhaul, just one board arranged their way, one report with their columns, one reminder worded like their own front desk would word it. Extension authoring exists for exactly that gap, and it is closer than most people think.\n\nAn Extension starts with a manifest, a short description of what it adds and what it needs permission to touch. Around that sits one file of code. That is genuinely the whole shape of it, and the authoring guides we are preparing walk through that shape line by line.\n\nWhen the draft works, you pack it. The result is a .fcxext file, a single file, the same pack format the marketplace already ships in. Install it from the file itself, on the desktop machine under the front desk or on the Android phone in the exam room. One tap, and the clinic has learned a new trick.\n\nKeep it for your own counters, or share it. A good number of Extensions in the marketplace began as one clinic's private fix for a Tuesday annoyance. Somebody else was having the same Tuesday. The marketplace is where those Tuesdays meet.\n\nWe will point to the guides the moment they land. Until then, the honest summary is this: the distance between an idea for your clinic and a working Extension is one manifest, one file, one tap. That is the shortest distance we know how to make, and we would rather clinics cross it than wait for us to guess their tricks for them."},{"id":"what-clinics-tell-us-at-setup","date":"2026-09-15","type":"note","category":"Community","title":"What clinics tell us at setup","excerpt":"When clinics write to us about setup, they rarely mention features first. They mention the first prescription their printer produced without argument, the first recall message that actually brought a patient back, the first screening camp where the tablet worked under a tent with no signal. Those three moments come up so often we have started to think of them as the real onboarding: not the installer, but the first three times the software kept its word.","tags":["community","clinics","feedback"],"body":""},{"id":"install-a-workbench-on-your-phone-in-one-tap","date":"2026-09-09","type":"blog","category":"Android","title":"Install a WorkBench on your phone in one tap","excerpt":"Open the .fcxwb file on your Android phone, tap once, and the whole WorkBench lands on the device. A clinic setup, carried in one file.","tags":["android","packs","workbench"],"body":"The exam room phone has become a serious tool. Optometrists carry them into refraction lanes, camp tents, and the occasional corridor consultation. So when we say a WorkBench now installs on Android in one tap, we mean it for the room where the work happens, not just the office.\n\nHere is the whole procedure. You receive a .fcxwb file, the same pack a colleague built on a desktop or pulled from the marketplace. You open it on the phone. You tap once. The WorkBench lands on the device with the clinic's own boards and workflow ready. There is no second app, no account shuffling, no typing a licence code character by character on a small keyboard.\n\nIt joins the rest of the pack family: Extensions as .fcxext, themes as .fcxtheme, capsules as .fcxcapsule, WorkBenches as .fcxwb. One-click install from the file itself, on desktop and Android alike. The file is the installer, which is a boring sentence that took a while to earn.\n\nWe watched a clinic set up a second room this way. The senior optometrist copied one file onto her phone at breakfast, opened it between patients, tapped, and was using the clinic's own board by the ten o'clock slot. Nobody called it a migration. It felt more like handing over a notebook.\n\nOne tap will not fix everything that is hard about running an eye clinic, but it removes a small tax we no longer believe clinics should pay. Carry the file. Open it. Tap. Get back to the patient in front of you."},{"id":"agents-and-the-console-one-licence-same-rules","date":"2026-09-03","type":"blog","category":"Automation","title":"Agents and the Console: one licence, same rules","excerpt":"The Console, the paired agents and the app all answer to the same licence and the same permissions. One clinic, many hands, one rulebook.","tags":["agents","automation","permissions"],"body":"Automation is only welcome if it knows its place. That sounds stern, but any clinic that has handed a task to software knows the worry: who is allowed to do this, and how would we know if something went too far. With the Console arriving in the terminal, we want to be plain about the answer.\n\nThe Console, the agents and the app all work under the same licence and the same permissions. Your licence code activates the clinic, the devices you trust in the admin console are the devices that may act, and an agent paired to a staff member carries that person's scopes, nothing more.\n\nIn practice that means the approval you set for a paired agent in the app is the same approval the Console respects when you run it from the terminal. A builder that drafts recall messages can act only as far as its scopes reach. There is one rulebook, and every hand that works the clinic reads from the same page.\n\nPicture the evening sweep: the optometrist pairs an agent to pull tomorrow's recall list, the front desk reviews it in the Console with the same permissions they hold in the app, and the admin console quietly keeps the account of who did what. Nobody memorises a second set of rules, because there is not one.\n\nWe think trust in automation is built from boring, readable rules rather than clever ones. One licence, trusted devices, scoped agents, visible approvals. When the Console ships in the terminal it will arrive under the same promise as everything else you already run: same licence, same permissions, same clinic."},{"id":"focuscasex-2-8-5-calmer-corners","date":"2026-08-29","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 2.8.5: calmer corners","excerpt":"2.8.5 is the small-steps release: quicker search across patient files, tidier print pages, and steadier pack installs on the machines clinics actually use.","tags":["release","optometry","offline"],"body":"Some releases announce themselves with a headline feature. This one announces itself with a quieter day at the counter. FocusCaseX 2.8.5 is out today, and it is made of the small fixes that decide whether a busy Saturday feels manageable or not.\n\nSearch comes first, because search is where every queue starts. Looking up a patient file is quicker across the board, including on the older machines many clinics keep running long after their prime. When the person at the desk can find the file while the patient is still taking off their sandals, the whole room relaxes.\n\nPrint pages got a tidy pass as well. Prescriptions, reports and camp sheets come out with margins that respect the printer and columns that stay where you put them. Clinics print a surprising amount of paper in a day, and paper that looks calm reads calm.\n\nPack installs are steadier too. The .fcx family, from Extensions to WorkBenches, lands with fewer interruptions on slow links, which matters for clinics that carry their setup between two rooms and a screening camp. A pack that installs in one tap should also behave once it is there.\n\nNone of this will make a keynote, and that is the point. 2.8.5 is the version we would want running on the machine under the front desk: unremarkable in the best sense, steady from the first patient to the last. Update when the day is done, and tell us what still feels rough. The corners we missed are already on our list."},{"id":"menus-you-can-drive-with-numbers","date":"2026-08-24","type":"note","category":"CLI","title":"Menus you can drive with numbers","excerpt":"Every list in the Console answers the keyboard: arrow keys to move a line at a time, or press 1-9 to jump straight where you mean to go, esc to step back a level, q to quit. It sounds small until you are standing at the front-desk machine with a stiff keyboard and a patient waiting, and the difference between nine presses and one is the difference between a queue and a calm afternoon.","tags":["cli","console","keyboard"],"body":""},{"id":"the-console-fcx-learns-a-home-screen","date":"2026-08-18","type":"blog","category":"CLI","title":"The Console: fcx learns a home screen","excerpt":"A preview of FCX CLI 3: type fcx with no arguments and the terminal opens a full-screen console with menus for setup, account, Extensions, WorkBenches and care.","tags":["cli","console","optometry"],"body":"Walk into almost any eye clinic we know and there is a machine that sits closest to the door: the front-desk terminal. It registers patients, prints tokens, checks recalls, and somehow also holds a jar of pens that nobody owns. For a long time, opening FocusCaseX from that machine meant remembering commands. Today we want to show you what we have been building instead.\n\nType fcx on its own and the terminal stops being a list of commands. It opens a full-screen console, a home for the whole clinic: menus for setup, for your account, for Extensions and WorkBenches, and for care. You do not memorise flags. You read the screen and pick a line.\n\nEvery list answers the keyboard. Arrow keys move you one line at a time, or press 1-9 to jump straight to the entry you already had in mind. Esc steps back a level, q quits. It is the kind of interface that suits a keyboard with worn keys and a queue at the counter.\n\nCare and diagnostics live in the console too, so the same screen that checks an installation can walk you through fixing it. And the marketplace browses from the terminal: Extensions and themes described in place, installed when you decide.\n\nThis is a preview of FCX CLI 3, and it ships soon as CLI 3.0. We are putting it in front of clinics now because the terminal has always been where optometry practices actually live, and we think software for eye care should meet people where they already stand. Try it, lean on it, tell us which menus feel wrong. A front door earns its name only after a lot of use."},{"id":"what-fcx-becomes-next","date":"2026-08-14","type":"blog","category":"Roadmap","title":"What fcx becomes next","excerpt":"A first look at what fcx becomes next: menus you can read at a glance, the clinic's care tools up front, and the marketplace next door — built for the terminal people.","tags":["roadmap","fcx","terminal"],"body":"Every few months a request stops being a request and becomes a shape. That happened this season with a certain kind of home for fcx: one place with menus you can read at a glance, the care tools a clinic reaches for all day, and the marketplace within arm's reach. We have started building it. Its name is the Console.\n\nIf you run fcx from the terminal today, you know the trade: fast, exact, and entirely on your shoulders to remember. What comes next keeps the speed and takes over the remembering — menus for the things you do weekly, keystrokes for the things you do hourly, and no ceremony between you and the workspace.\n\nCare tools come first. The clinic's daily work moves to the front: recalls, dispensing lists, camp days, the boards you already know — reachable without memorising anything. The marketplace settles in next door, so trying a new Extension becomes a walk across the room rather than a separate errand.\n\nWe are not retiring the terminal — the terminal is the point. The new home is for the days when you want the software to show its hand instead of asking you to recall it, and for the colleague who never learned the commands. Same room, same files, same offline rules.\n\nNo dates to promise, no screenshots to tease — just the shape, and an invitation: tell us what the first menus should hold. Write to us the way you always do; the requests that shaped the Android app are the ones that will shape this. The bench is cleared. This is what we are building next."},{"id":"the-case-for-keeping-records-on-the-device","date":"2026-08-12","type":"blog","category":"Security","title":"The case for keeping records on the device","excerpt":"Privacy as architecture: records live on the device, the licence belongs to the clinic, and the network is optional. Why we would not build FocusCaseX any other way.","tags":["security","privacy","offline-emr"],"body":"Ask us why patient records live on the device and not in a rented corner of someone else's computer, and you will get a longer answer than you expected. Not because the answer is complicated, but because it is the one conviction the whole product stands on.\n\nPrivacy is usually discussed as a policy — promises in a document, reviewed once a year. We prefer it as architecture. If a chart never leaves the device, there is no pipeline to tap and no trophy file sitting on a far-away machine with someone else's keys in the lock. The strongest privacy promise we can make is a place the data never goes.\n\nThe licence follows the same instinct. Your FCX-XXXXXX code belongs to the clinic, your devices are the trusted ones you named, and the workspace runs because you run it — not because a distant status page says you may. When the network is optional, so is everyone standing between you and your own records.\n\nThis is not nostalgia for hard drives; it is a judgement about whose machine holds the most sensitive paper in an eye clinic. The refraction of a schoolchild, the pressures of a glaucoma patient, the address of a family that prefers to be unlisted — those belong on a device the optometrist owns, in a room she locks at night.\n\nOffline-first gets described as a convenience, a way to survive bad networks. It is that, but it is also the privacy posture: the network optional, the records local, the clinic sovereign. We would not build it any other way, and we will keep saying so in plain words."},{"id":"back-to-school-vision-checks-without-the-queue","date":"2026-08-07","type":"note","category":"Eye Health","title":"Back-to-school vision checks, without the queue","excerpt":"School season is the volume test: the recall board carries the follow-ups, the camp days carry the screening, and the night before carries the rest. Print the class lists, the recall slips and the blank refraction templates before you sleep — the morning queue will thank you.","tags":["eye-health","school-screening","recalls"],"body":""},{"id":"a-weekend-camp-zero-bars-of-signal","date":"2026-08-02","type":"blog","category":"Clinic Story","title":"A weekend camp, zero bars of signal","excerpt":"A vision camp at the edge of town, zero bars all day: registration to printed prescription on tablets, synced to nothing because nothing needed syncing.","tags":["vision-camps","android","clinic-story"],"body":"The hall was a school building at the edge of town, the kind with one fan per room and a stone floor that keeps the morning cool. We arrived before the queue did, and the first thing we checked was not the equipment — it was the phone. Zero bars. Not one, all day. Outside, the town had its usual Sunday hum; inside, the day would run on its own power.\n\nNobody panicked, because the day did not need the bars. Registration ran on the tablets: new charts made on the spot, names entered at the bench, each record living on the device in front of the volunteer. The camp board came up on the tablet by the door, moving as patients moved — waiting, examined, sent to the dispensing bench. A volunteer with one afternoon of training ran the bench without help.\n\nRefractions went the way they go in any busy hall, except every finding went straight into the chart on the tablet instead of onto a slip of paper. By noon the hall was full and the boards were keeping order better than the megaphone usually does.\n\nThe prescriptions printed at the counter table, from the tablets, the way they would have printed in a clinic with full signal — because the printer, like everything else that mattered, was not waiting on the outside world. Synced to nothing, because nothing needed syncing.\n\nWe drove back at dusk with every chart safely on the devices that made them. The camp had run on batteries, patience and an offline-first EMR, in that order. The signal came back somewhere past the market — nobody noticed, and nobody needed to."},{"id":"the-two-screen-day-phone-and-laptop-together","date":"2026-07-31","type":"note","category":"Workflow Tips","title":"The two-screen day: phone and laptop together","excerpt":"The two-screen day is our favourite way to work: the phone walks the exam room and the laptop holds the desk. Same files, same licence, same boards — the refraction starts in your hand and finishes at the counter without a word spoken. The day never stops moving between the two, and neither should you.","tags":["workflow","android","tips"],"body":""},{"id":"fcx-open-the-workspace-on-your-own-machine","date":"2026-07-28","type":"blog","category":"CLI","title":"fcx open: the workspace on your own machine","excerpt":"fcx open serves the workspace on the machine itself — a local web app in your browser with no internet needed, and it answers on the clinic network too when one exists.","tags":["cli","offline-emr","eye-clinic-app"],"body":"The best commands are the short ones, and fcx open is short on purpose: it serves your workspace on the machine itself and opens it in the browser. No internet involved. The web app it hands the browser is local — the pages come from the same machine that holds your charts, not from somewhere across the sea.\n\nType fcx open on the clinic's own desktop and the workspace appears in a browser tab, the boards live, the charts where you left them. Because it is served by the machine, it also answers on the clinic network when one exists — the tablet in the exam room can reach it over the local network, no internet required, no cloud consulted.\n\nWhy a web app at all, when there is a desktop app and an Android app? Because sometimes the browser is simply the most honest place to work from: the reception machine that cannot install anything, the borrowed laptop at a camp, the second screen at the dispensing counter. fcx open meets the day where the day already is.\n\nAnd because everything it shows comes from the machine itself, the offline rule holds. The internet can be down, the router can be off, the broadband can be unpaid — the tab still opens. For clinics that keep their records on their own devices, this is the same promise wearing a different shape.\n\nfcx open is in the current release. Run it from the terminal you already use for everything else, or let it open the workspace for you — either way, the machine you already own becomes the place your workspace lives."},{"id":"the-catalog-crosses-ninety","date":"2026-07-23","type":"note","category":"Extensions","title":"The catalog crosses ninety","excerpt":"The catalog crossed ninety items this week: Extensions, capsules, themes, templates, snippets and WorkBenches. Almost none of it came from a roadmap written in a room — it came from optometrists writing to us about the thing their counter needed on a Tuesday. Ninety is a number we will forget; the requests behind it, we keep.","tags":["extensions","catalog","marketplace"],"body":""},{"id":"the-solo-optometrist-workbench","date":"2026-07-18","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"The Solo Optometrist WorkBench","excerpt":"One install for a solo practice: nine capsules, five Extensions, five templates, four snippets and one theme, all in a single .fcxwb — a full optometry workspace in one step.","tags":["workbench","solo-practice","announcement"],"body":"Some requests arrive once and some arrive every week until we give in. The Solo Optometrist WorkBench has been the second kind, and today it is here: one install, built for the practice of one — the optometrist who examines, dispenses, keeps the books and closes the shutter at night.\n\nOpen the .fcxwb file, confirm, and a complete optometry workspace comes up on the first screen: nine capsules, five Extensions, five templates, four snippets and one theme. The boards a solo day actually runs on — today's patients, refractions waiting, dispensing due, recalls owed — are alive before you have configured anything, because there is nothing to configure.\n\nEvery capsule in it earned its place. We spent months with solo practices, watching what a one-person clinic actually opens on a working day, and the answer shaped the bundle as much as the requests did. What a solo optometrist does not need turned out to matter as much as what she does; several ideas we liked were left on the bench.\n\nThe five Extensions are the small features that matter most when there is no one to cover for you: the counter-side conveniences, the camp-ready list tools, the little time-savers that add up across a long day. The templates carry the refraction patterns solo days repeat. The theme is set for long days and small screens, because solo often means tablet.\n\nIf you are opening your own practice this season, start here rather than from a blank workspace — the Solo WorkBench is one file, one step, and then you are open. And if you have been running on paper since before we existed, this is the gentlest door in. Write to us and tell us what your first week with it looks like; that is how the next bundle gets built."},{"id":"the-workbench-library-grows","date":"2026-07-13","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"The WorkBench library grows","excerpt":"Whole clinic setups, curated and installable in one step — capsules, Extensions, templates, snippets and a theme in a single .fcxwb. The library is now the fastest way to start.","tags":["workbench","capsules","extensions","optometry-software"],"body":"Setting up clinic software used to start with a blank screen and an afternoon. We think starting should feel like walking into a room that is already set up for you. That is the idea behind WorkBenches, and this month the library behind that idea has grown into the fastest way we know to begin.\n\nA WorkBench is a whole clinic setup in one file: the capsules that render your boards, the Extensions that add the small features your counter needs, the templates your refractions start from, the snippets your team retypes all day long, and a theme that makes it all read like your clinic. One install and the workspace is not empty — it is occupied, arranged, and already working.\n\nThe library now covers the kinds of practices we hear from most: the single-room optometry clinic, the dispense-heavy counter, the camp team that needs boards a volunteer can read at a glance. Each WorkBench is curated rather than assembled — we sit with the workflows, argue about the order of things, and only then bundle it.\n\nInstalling one takes the same step every pack takes: open the file, confirm, done. On desktop or on Android, the .fcxwb lands in your workspace and the boards come up live. Nothing to configure first, no checklist to follow before you can see your own day.\n\nIf you are starting a practice, or restarting one after years of paper, try beginning from a WorkBench instead of a blank page. More are on the bench right now, shaped by the requests that arrive every week. The blank screen is losing."},{"id":"focuscasex-2-8-the-android-release","date":"2026-07-08","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 2.8: the Android release","excerpt":"FocusCaseX 2.8 is out: pack installs by opening the file on Android, calmer boards for small screens, and the small mobile details that hands notice first.","tags":["release","android","focuscasex"],"body":"Release day, and this one belongs to the pocket. FocusCaseX 2.8 is out today, and the centre of gravity is the Android app — the version of the workspace that lives on phones and tablets and moves with the optometrist through exam rooms, counters and camps.\n\nThe headline change: packs now install on Android by opening the file itself. A .fcxwb WorkBench pack, a .fcxcapsule, a .fcxtheme, a .fcxext Extension — share the file to the device, open it, confirm, done. No marketplace detour, no desktop round trip. The pack family behaves the same everywhere we ship it.\n\nBoards are calmer in 2.8. Live boards rendered from capsules read better on a phone-sized screen: steadier spacing, clearer headings, fewer things competing for the eye. On a small screen, restraint is a feature.\n\nThen there are the little things mobile hands notice, the ones that never make a changelog headline. Bigger touch targets where thumbs actually land. Fields that keep the keyboard from covering what you are typing. Charts that scroll the way a chart should. Most of them came straight from notes sent to us by optometrists using the app in real rooms. None of these will photograph well, and all of them will be felt within a minute.\n\nThe app will tell you itself when 2.8 is ready — the update notice appears inside the app, and you can install it whenever the room is empty. If you practise from a tablet, this is the release we have been building toward since the first day we carried one into an exam room."},{"id":"a-full-offline-day-measured","date":"2026-07-03","type":"blog","category":"Android","title":"A full offline day, measured","excerpt":"A whole clinic day with the router off — registrations, refractions, dispensing, recalls — and why offline-first is the whole product, not a mode.","tags":["offline-emr","android","eye-clinic"],"body":"Before the first patient arrived, we switched the router off. Not a dropped connection, not a bad patch of signal — off, deliberately, for a whole day of registrations, refractions, dispensing and recalls. We have said for a long time that FocusCaseX is offline-first, and we wanted to write down what that actually feels like from the first walk-in to the last recall.\n\nMorning registration ran the way it does on any busy Tuesday. New patients, a few walk-ins off the early bus, a schoolchild with her grandmother asking about the upcoming camp. Every chart opened the moment it was asked for, because every record was already on the device. Nothing was being fetched from anywhere; there was nowhere to fetch from.\n\nBy late morning the refraction bench was deep and the boards were doing their steady work. The capsule boards still rendered live on the tablet at the side of the room — today's list, pending dispensing, this week's recalls — moving as the day moved, no network consulted. The optometrist worked from the tablet; the front desk worked from the same day's list. At no point did anyone ask whether we were online. That question had simply left the building.\n\nThe afternoon was dispensing and printing, and this is where paper clinics usually leak. Prescriptions printed from the tablet at the counter. A recall for a glaucoma follow-up went onto next month's board before the patient reached the door. When the owner asked what we had lost by being offline all day, the honest answer was: the chance to find out.\n\nHere is the conclusion we keep arriving at, and the reason we build the way we build: offline-first is not a mode you switch to when the network fails. It is the whole product, the default ground the day stands on. For an eye clinic in a market lane or a camp at the edge of town, the network is a guest. The software should treat it that way."},{"id":"updates-inside-the-app-never-a-hunt","date":"2026-06-28","type":"note","category":"Android","title":"Updates inside the app, never a hunt","excerpt":"The Android app checks for its own updates and tells you inside the app when one is ready — no scavenger hunt through download pages, no forwarded files on a chat thread, no version numbers to guess. The notice waits at the top of your day; you tap it when the room is empty, and the app does the rest. If you have ever postponed an update because hunting it down was harder than living without it, this is the fix.","tags":["android","updates","eye-clinic-app"],"body":""},{"id":"the-clinic-in-your-pocket","date":"2026-06-23","type":"blog","category":"Android","title":"The clinic in your pocket","excerpt":"The same charts, boards and packs on your phone and tablet — carried into second rooms, camps and home visits. Records live on the device; the network stays optional.","tags":["android","optometry-software","offline-emr"],"body":"There is a moment in every camp day when the desk is too far away. The patient is seated, the trial frame is settled on the nose, and the register lies somewhere across the hall. For years the answer was a paper slip and a walk back. This month we are putting the whole workspace somewhere better: FocusCaseX now runs on your phone and your tablet, and it carries the clinic with it.\n\nThe Android app is not a viewer or a stripped-down companion. It holds the same charts you open at the desk, the same live boards rendered from capsules, the same packs you installed with one tap. A refraction that starts on the tablet in the exam room finishes at the counter without a retype, because it is one workspace living in both places, not two that need to catch up with each other.\n\nThe part that matters most is quiet: records live on the device. The network is optional. In a basement second room, at a home visit across town, in a camp hall where the signal comes and goes like the power, the chart opens anyway. Nothing about the day waits on a spinner.\n\nWe built this for the way eye care actually moves here — the main clinic, the branch across the market, the Sunday vision camp, the follow-up at a patient's home. One licence, trusted devices, the same files wherever you practise. An optometrist should not have to choose between the room she is standing in and the software she trusts. It is optometry software that follows the optometrist, not the other way around.\n\nIf you carry a tablet into the exam room this week, try it with the network off. That is the test we run every day on our own benches, and it is the only one we think counts."},{"id":"teaching-a-room-of-students-with-one-pack","date":"2026-06-19","type":"note","category":"Community","title":"Teaching a room of students with one pack","excerpt":"An optometry class we heard about starts the term the calm way: the teacher shares one .fcxwb with the room, every student opens the same file on their own device, and the whole class begins on identical workspaces. Same starter pages, same capsules, same theme. Nobody spends the morning matching screens; they are practising refraction instead.","tags":["classroom","workbench","teaching"],"body":""},{"id":"from-the-marketplace-to-a-file-and-back","date":"2026-06-15","type":"blog","category":"Extensions","title":"From the marketplace to a file and back","excerpt":"Marketplace items and pack files are two doors to the same place. Install by code when browsing, carry the file when sharing — the choice stays with the clinic.","tags":["marketplace","packs","extensions"],"body":"Which is better: fetching an Extension from the marketplace, or receiving it as a file from someone you trust? We hear this question at demos, and the honest answer is that they are the same thing wearing different coats. In FocusCaseX, marketplace items and pack files are two doors into one room, and the choice of door belongs to the clinic.\n\nThe marketplace door suits discovery. You browse what other eye clinics rely on, read how an Extension describes itself, and install with a click or by code. It is how most clinics meet their first Extension, and how a front desk finds the board it did not know it wanted.\n\nThe file door suits everything after discovery. The branch across town sends a .fcxext in a chat. A senior optometrist hands a new colleague a .fcxtheme at a conference. A camp coordinator carries .fcxcapsule files on a tablet that sees no signal for days. In every case the pack installs straight from the file itself, declares its contents first, and leaves cleanly when removed. No marketplace round trip is required, and none is forbidden either.\n\nSince 2.7, the doors connect in both directions. A capsule discovered on the marketplace in March can be a file in a cousin clinic's inbox by June. An Extension that arrived as a .fcxext can send a curious colleague back to the marketplace to see what else is worth trying. Nothing gets stuck on either side.\n\nWe keep both doors open because eye care runs on both patterns: the trusted recommendation and the independent browse. Install by code when you are browsing. Carry the file when the network is nowhere or the colleague is standing right there. The choice stays where it always belonged, with the clinic."},{"id":"back-up-your-setup-as-one-fcxwb","date":"2026-06-10","type":"blog","category":"Workflow Tips","title":"Back up your setup as one .fcxwb","excerpt":"The Friday habit: export your setup as one .fcxwb beside your backups. The whole workspace — Extensions, boards, theme, starter pages — rebuilds from a single file, in one step.","tags":["backup","workbench","packs"],"body":"Habits survive when they are small. Backing up has a reputation for being large, which is why most clinics mean to do it and many keep postponing it. So here is a smaller version: on Friday, before you lock the front desk, export your setup as one .fcxwb and drop the file in with the rest of your backup.\n\nA .fcxwb is a whole WorkBench in a single file: Extensions, capsules, a theme, templates and starter pages. That is not a folder of documents. It is the shape of the clinic's working day, packed. If a machine fails, a tablet is replaced, or you are standing in a new branch facing an empty install, the workspace returns from that one file in one step.\n\nPatient records have their own care routine, and a pack is not a replacement for it. The .fcxwb covers everything around the records instead: the boards the front desk leans on, the vitals layout in the exam room, the theme that makes a screen feel like yours. Rebuilding those by hand costs an evening; reinstalling them from a file costs a tap.\n\nThe habit scales beyond disasters, too. Moving to a new laptop, setting up a visiting optometrist's desk for a month, preparing the second branch: all the same gesture. Install the .fcxwb, adjust what you like, carry on. Some clinics keep a small shelf of packs — camp season, festival week, the everyday setup — and change into whichever one the calendar calls for.\n\nTry it this Friday. Export once, look at the file sitting beside your backups, and notice how small a whole clinic setup looks when it is one pack. That smallness is the point. A setup you can carry is a setup you will actually keep safe."},{"id":"focuscasex-2-7-the-pack-release","date":"2026-06-05","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 2.7: the pack release","excerpt":"In 2.7, pack installs work everywhere — app, site, terminal, phone — and marketplace items can become files. The pack family is now the way FocusCaseX setups travel.","tags":["release","packs","focuscasex"],"body":"Release notes are usually a list. This one is a shape. FocusCaseX 2.7 is the version where packs went everywhere, so we are calling it the pack release: the .fcx family that arrived in April now installs from every surface a clinic actually uses.\n\nConcretely, that means install from the app on a laptop, install from the site when a pack arrives by email, install from a terminal with fcx install, and install on a phone by opening the file and tapping. Same pack, same declaration of contents, same one-click finish. A clinic manager can receive a .fcxext on any device she owns and trust that the install behaves the same on all of them.\n\nThe marketplace learned the same trick in this release. Items there can now become files: browse as always, then take an Extension away as a .fcxext, a theme as a .fcxtheme, a set of capsules as a .fcxcapsule, a whole WorkBench as a .fcxwb. The marketplace stays the front door for discovery; packs are how setups travel once chosen. Two doors, one house.\n\nEverything else in 2.7 serves that idea. Install screens that state their contents clearly. Removal that takes a pack out whole. Quick installs on Android phones and tablets. And the offline behaviour you already rely on — records on the device, the network optional — now extended to every pack you bring in.\n\n2.7 is reaching clinics now. If a colleague has been meaning to try the Extension you keep recommending, this is a good week to send the file instead of the instructions."},{"id":"what-a-pack-file-can-and-cannot-do","date":"2026-06-03","type":"blog","category":"Security","title":"What a pack file can and cannot do","excerpt":"A pack declares what it carries before it installs, the install shows exactly what it touches, and removal takes it all back out. Nothing runs invisibly.","tags":["security","packs","trust"],"body":"Every install is an act of trust, and we would rather earn it in the open than ask for it in the dark. That is the thinking behind how pack files behave in FocusCaseX. A pack — a .fcxext, a .fcxtheme, a .fcxcapsule or a .fcxwb — declares its contents before it installs: the Extensions it brings, the boards it will paste, the settings it will touch. You read the list before anything happens.\n\nThe install screen then shows exactly what the pack touches. Nothing runs invisibly. If a capsule wants a place on the front desk, the screen says so. If a theme will re-skin every page, the screen says that too. Approval is a deliberate tap, with fcx install on the desktop and the same declaration in front of you on Android.\n\nWhat a pack cannot do matters just as much. It stays inside the lines it declared at install time. It does not quietly grow new parts afterwards, and it does not reach into patient records beyond what your roles and the admin console already allow. A pack is a guest that introduces itself at the door and stays where it was invited.\n\nLeaving is as honest as arriving. Removal takes the whole pack back out — the Extension, its boards, its settings — rather than a residue of half-configured pieces. A clinic that tries a pack for a week is not making a permanent decision, which keeps experimenting inexpensive and safe.\n\nOffline-first is a promise that your records live on your device. Packs make the same kind of promise about their own behaviour: declared first, visible throughout, removable in one step. That is the standard we will hold every file in the .fcx family to, on every device a clinic runs."},{"id":"two-branches-one-file-each","date":"2026-05-31","type":"note","category":"Clinic Story","title":"Two branches, one file each","excerpt":"The two-branch clinic we wrote about last year has a new habit. Setting up the second room took one .fcxwb: the first branch's setup exported as a single file, carried across, installed in one step. Same Extensions, same boards, same theme, and the new desk felt like the old one before the first patient arrived.","tags":["branches","workbench","setup"],"body":""},{"id":"quick-install-on-android-tap-the-pack-it-loads","date":"2026-05-26","type":"blog","category":"Android","title":"Quick install on Android: tap the pack, it loads","excerpt":"On the Android app, opening a pack file installs it — one tap from download to working feature, on phones and tablets, online or off. The single-file feel clinics asked for.","tags":["android","packs","install"],"body":"At a screening camp, the table holds a tablet, a paper ledger, and whatever the signal decides to be that day. That setting is exactly why we have made pack installs quick on Android. On a phone or tablet running the FocusCaseX app, opening a pack file installs it: one tap from download to working feature.\n\nThe Android app has been offline-first since it arrived earlier this year: records live on the device, and the network stays optional. Packs follow the same rule. The file might sit in your downloads, your email or a chat; you open it, the app shows what the pack carries, you confirm, and the Extension or board or theme is in. If the camp ground has no signal at all, nothing about that sequence changes.\n\nThis is the single-file feel clinics kept asking for. Managers told us they wanted setups they could pass along the way they pass along a photo: tap it, it opens, it works. A pack file behaves that way now, whether it is a .fcxext for the camp team or a .fcxtheme for the festival-week look.\n\nNothing runs invisibly on a phone either. The pack declares its contents first — the Extensions, boards and settings it brings — and the install screen shows exactly what it touches before you approve. When you remove it later, removal takes it all back out.\n\nTablets at the front desk, phones in the refraction lane, a camp coordinator passing packs between devices at the end of the day. This is how eye care actually runs across India, and now installs keep up with it."},{"id":"capsules-that-travel-the-fcxcapsule","date":"2026-05-21","type":"blog","category":"Extensions","title":"Capsules that travel: the .fcxcapsule","excerpt":"Appointments for the front desk, vitals for the exam room: the boards a clinic loves now travel as one .fcxcapsule and land as live boards in the next workspace, in a tap.","tags":["capsules","packs","boards"],"body":"Every clinic has boards it cannot work without. The front desk leans on the appointments board the way a shopkeeper leans on her ledger, and the exam room keeps the vitals board open all afternoon. Capsules are how those boards get pasted into a workspace, and from this week they travel: a .fcxcapsule carries the boards a clinic loves as one file.\n\nWatch it happen at a counter. The receptionist at one eye clinic keeps her morning view arranged just so: today's appointments up top, the recall list beside them, nothing she does not need. When a colleague joins the second branch, she sends a .fcxcapsule across in a chat. The new desk opens the file, taps install, and the front desk looks like home before the first patient walks in.\n\nThe same gesture works for exam rooms. A vitals capsule with exactly the fields a refractionist uses, passed from one table to another, saves the second clinic an afternoon of rebuilding from memory. And because capsules render live boards, what arrives is not a picture of a workflow. It is the workflow, updating as the day moves.\n\nLike every pack, a .fcxcapsule declares what it carries before it installs, and the install screen shows what it touches. If a board eventually outlives its usefulness, removal takes it back out whole. The pack carries everything it needs, so none of this waits on a network or a login.\n\nCapsules were always the quickest way to make FocusCaseX feel like your clinic. Now they are the quickest thing to hand over."},{"id":"fcxtheme-a-coat-of-paint-in-one-file","date":"2026-05-16","type":"note","category":"Feature","title":".fcxtheme: a coat of paint in one file","excerpt":"Themes join the pack family. A .fcxtheme is a coat of paint in a single file: every surface of the clinic workspace re-skinned, from the front-desk board to the exam-room pages. It installs the same one-click way every pack does, and removal takes the paint back out again.","tags":["themes","packs","install"],"body":""},{"id":"workbenches-go-portable-meet-fcxwb","date":"2026-05-11","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"WorkBenches go portable: meet .fcxwb","excerpt":"A whole WorkBench — Extensions, capsules, a theme, templates, starter pages — now travels as one .fcxwb and installs in a single step, at the next desk or the next branch.","tags":["workbench","packs","setup"],"body":"A WorkBench is the most complete thing FocusCaseX knows how to assemble: Extensions, capsules, a theme, templates and starter pages, installed in one step. Until April it lived only where you put it. Now it travels, because a whole WorkBench fits inside one .fcxwb file.\n\nPicture the manager of a two-branch eye clinic, the kind we meet at every screening camp. She wants the second room to feel like the first: the same recall board at the front desk, the same vitals capsule in the exam room, the same colours on every screen. A few weeks ago that meant an evening of clicking through each piece in order. Now she exports the WorkBench as a .fcxwb, copies it across, and installs it at the other branch in one step.\n\nInside the pack sits everything the setup needs, declared before it installs. The second branch sees the list, approves it with a tap, and the workspace arrives whole. Anyone who has worked a shift at the first branch recognises the second one immediately, right down to where the appointment board sits.\n\nThat is also how setups move between people now. A senior optometrist winding down a practice can hand a younger colleague a .fcxwb instead of a folder of instructions. A trainer can prepare one standard workspace and hand it to every fresh install. The file carries the habits, not just the tools.\n\nWe keep saying files are becoming the way setups travel. The .fcxwb is the clearest proof: an entire clinic workspace, from starter pages to theme, carried in one file and installed in one step, offline or not."},{"id":"install-straight-from-the-file-itself","date":"2026-05-06","type":"blog","category":"Extensions","title":"Install straight from the file itself","excerpt":"Hand fcx install a pack file and it installs from the file alone; on Android, open the pack and tap. The pack carries everything it needs, wherever your clinic works.","tags":["install","packs","desktop"],"body":"Two optometrists stood at the counter of a clinic we visit often, one holding a phone, the other a laptop, both staring at the same small file. The question was practical: does installing this need the marketplace, a login, and a quiet evening? Since the pack family arrived, the answer is no. A pack installs straight from the file itself, wherever the file happens to be sitting.\n\nOn the desktop, fcx install is the whole ritual. Hand it the file, whether it is a .fcxext, a .fcxtheme, a .fcxcapsule or a .fcxwb, and it installs what the pack carries. There is no stage where the software wanders off to fetch something else first, because the pack carries everything it needs. That is why a pack behaves identically at a clinic with steady broadband and at one that runs offline most of the week.\n\nOn Android it is shorter still. Open the file on a phone or tablet, tap install, and the Extension, theme, capsule or WorkBench lands in the app. For optometrists who move between a morning screening camp and an afternoon at the shop, that shape matters: the network is optional, so the install works wherever the file already is.\n\nBefore anything lands, the pack shows its hand: the Extensions, boards and settings it brings. Nothing runs invisibly, and the person tapping install knows exactly what is being added. When a clinic later decides a pack has outlived its usefulness, removal takes it all back out.\n\nThat is the promise of the pack family in one sentence: the file is the installer. Keep a few in a folder, copy them to the branch, email one to a colleague across town. It loads from wherever it sits, and the clinic gets on with the day."},{"id":"fcxext-an-extension-you-can-email","date":"2026-05-02","type":"note","category":"Feature","title":".fcxext: an Extension you can email","excerpt":"An Extension used to be something you fetched; now it is something you hand over. A .fcxext is one file, so the clinic next door can send it across the road, and you can open it on your phone and tap install. No marketplace round trip, no guesswork.","tags":["extensions","packs","files"],"body":""},{"id":"one-file-one-click-the-fcx-pack-family","date":"2026-04-28","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"One file, one click: the .fcx pack family","excerpt":"Setups travel as packs now: .fcxext for Extensions, .fcxtheme for themes, .fcxcapsule for boards, .fcxwb for a whole WorkBench. One file, one click, on desktop and Android.","tags":["packs","extensions","install"],"body":"Launch days at CaseX Labs usually begin with a long checklist. Today's list is short: four new letters, four kinds of file, one click. Starting this morning, FocusCaseX setups travel as packs. A .fcxext is an Extension as one file, a .fcxtheme is a theme as one file, a .fcxcapsule carries capsule boards, and a .fcxwb carries a whole WorkBench. Each one is a single file a clinic can send, keep and install on its own terms.\n\nWe built this for the way eye care actually moves. A refractionist hears about a handy appointment board from a colleague in another town. A clinic manager wants the exam room at the second branch to feel like the first. A lecturer wants every student bench in a college lab to look alike. Until this week, each of those wishes meant walking someone through a marketplace, screen by screen. Now it can be a file in a chat, an attachment in an email, a copy on a pen drive.\n\nInstalling stays deliberately boring. On the desktop, hand the file to fcx install and it does the rest. On the Android app, open the pack and tap install, the same gesture as opening a photo. Before anything lands, the pack declares what it carries, so you always see the Extensions, boards and settings you are letting in.\n\nNothing about the marketplace goes away. It remains the front door for browsing and for discovering what other eye clinics rely on. Packs are the other door, for the setups you already trust and want to hand over whole. And because FocusCaseX is offline-first through and through, a pack behaves the same on a machine that never touches the network that day. Records live on the device either way.\n\nFour letters, then: .fcxext, .fcxtheme, .fcxcapsule, .fcxwb. You will be seeing them a lot from now on, usually attached to a message that says, try this at your counter. We hope you will pass a few along yourself."},{"id":"next-the-clinic-in-one-file","date":"2026-04-24","type":"blog","category":"Roadmap","title":"Next: the clinic in one file","excerpt":"What we are building next: packs — Extensions, themes, capsules, whole workspace setups bundled as single files you can hold, send and install. The clinic in one file.","tags":["roadmap","packs","extensions"],"body":"The next thing on our board is small to say and big to hand over: packs. A pack is one file that carries a piece of FocusCaseX inside it — an extension, a theme, a capsule, or a whole workspace setup — and installs wherever you send it. We are calling the idea what it is: the clinic in one file.\n\nEverything in that list already exists. Extensions come from the marketplace. Themes, templates and snippets are one-file customizations today. Capsules render live boards wherever they are pasted. WorkBenches set up a whole clinic in one install. What is new is the wrapping: instead of arranging these piece by piece, you hold them as files, send one to another machine, keep one in a drawer, hand one to the practice opening across town.\n\nWhy files: because eye care runs on things you can hold. A clinic that has tuned its camp workflow into capsules and templates should be able to carry that tuning to the second machine without retyping any of it. A team covering a district screening camp should be able to carry the setup on a pen drive, not a checklist. Files need a hand, not a meeting.\n\nThat is the whole preview for now. Packs are next on the roadmap, and the shapes above are the first ones they will take. We are not ready to show the details, and we would rather arrive late and whole than early and half-built. When packs land, they will land the way this product always has: plain, portable, and yours to pass around.\n\nThe clinic in one file is the sentence on our whiteboard. The next few months will tell us whether we earned it. We think we will."},{"id":"opening-the-second-room-without-buying-twice","date":"2026-04-19","type":"blog","category":"Clinic Story","title":"Opening the second room without buying twice","excerpt":"A clinic adds a second exam room and asks: do we buy everything twice. One more code, the same workspace, and the day runs across both rooms without a data divide.","tags":["growth","devices","clinics"],"body":"Picture a clinic with one refraction table, a waiting bench that fills by eleven, and a lease just signed on the room next door. The space was the good news. The first question was the one every growing practice asks: does the second room need its own copy of everything, from records to licences to software?\n\nThe answer is no, and it takes one sentence. A second room is a second device, not a second clinic. The owner issues one more activation code — the device limit is two by default, and there is room to grow when the clinic needs it — the new machine activates, and it is trusted like the first. Same workspace, same owner's email, one more seat.\n\nBecause the workspace is offline-first, each machine keeps its records on the device, ready through the afternoon power cut and the camp-day dead zones. When the network allows, the two rooms catch up with each other, and a fcx sync at closing leaves both machines agreeing on the day. The morning reads as one morning, not two piles of files to reconcile.\n\nWhat the owner noticed first was the absence of new decisions. No second licence to negotiate, no cheaper tier for the side room, no debate about which machine holds the true copy of the records. The plan already covered the clinic; the code simply gave the room a seat. And when both devices appeared side by side on the Devices page, named and checked in, it read like an inventory of the practice itself — the kind of list an owner keeps in their head, now written down somewhere they can see it.\n\nGrowth should feel like furniture, not paperwork. One more code, one more trusted device, the same complete workspace underneath both rooms. That is how a clinic doubles its space without buying itself twice."},{"id":"issuing-codes-without-the-wait","date":"2026-04-16","type":"note","category":"Workflow Tips","title":"Issuing codes without the wait","excerpt":"A new computer on a Monday morning used to mean a support ticket and a wait; now the owner opens the Devices page, issues a code, and reads it to the new machine across the counter. Activation takes minutes, and the seat moves when the clinic wants it to. The inbox never gets involved.","tags":["workflow","activation","tips"],"body":""},{"id":"what-the-licence-check-sends-and-what-it-never-does","date":"2026-04-14","type":"blog","category":"Security","title":"What the licence check sends, and what it never does","excerpt":"The licence check confirms a device is trusted and does one thing only. It never reads your records. What crosses the network, what stays home, and why it matters.","tags":["security","privacy","licensing"],"body":"A fair question follows any mention of a licence check: what exactly is being sent, and who sees it. It deserves a plain answer, because an offline-first product lives or dies by what it keeps on the device. Here is ours.\n\nThe licence check does one thing. It confirms the device is trusted, meaning the activation code you entered belongs to this clinic and the machine is on your device list. That is the entire conversation. What crosses the network is licence status, nothing more: trusted or not, and a seat accounted for. No charts travel with it.\n\nWhat it never does is read records. Patient files are plain files on the device, and no licence event opens them, moves them, or sums them up. The registry checks the code; the clinic keeps the charts. That boundary between the two is the design, not a promise bolted on afterwards.\n\nThe difference matters because trust in eye clinic software should fit in a sentence. The licence is about the device. The records are about the patients. When those two stay apart, a clinic can let a machine check in without wondering what left the building with it.\n\nIt is also why a trusted device keeps working offline without anxiety. The check is small and occasional; the workspace is local and complete. Cut the network and the clinic still runs. Bring it back and the licence catches up in its own time. That is offline-first, applied to licensing by the same rule as everything else."},{"id":"what-if-the-clinic-laptop-dies","date":"2026-04-09","type":"note","category":"Community","title":"What if the clinic laptop dies?","excerpt":"'What happens if the laptop dies' is the question owners ask before anything else. The answer is three calm steps: revoke the lost machine from the Devices page, issue a fresh code for the new one, and restore the records from the backup you already keep. The licence moves in minutes; the clinic keeps its history.","tags":["community","backup","devices"],"body":""},{"id":"three-plans-one-complete-workspace","date":"2026-04-04","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Three plans, one complete workspace","excerpt":"Monthly ₹399, Quarterly ₹1,299, Yearly ₹4,499 — every plan the complete FocusCaseX, nothing capped, taxes included, UPI, cards, net banking.","tags":["pricing","plans","india"],"body":"Pricing pages usually hide the catch. Ours has a different problem: there is no catch to hide. From today, FocusCaseX comes in three plans — Monthly at ₹399, Quarterly at ₹1,299, Yearly at ₹4,499 — and every one of them is the complete workspace.\n\nComplete means complete. Clinical pages as plain files, capsules that render live boards, the marketplace and its extensions, themes, templates and snippets, WorkBenches, the fcx command, the licensing tools with the admin console. Nothing is capped: no patient ceilings, no feature tiers, no asterisks in a footnote. The only difference between the plans is the rhythm of payment.\n\nTaxes are included in the number you see, so the price on the page is the price on the invoice, which is how a clinic budget should work. Payment runs the way Indian clinics actually pay: UPI, cards, net banking. Nothing special to arrange before starting, and the yearly plan is there for owners who prefer to settle it once and think about other things.\n\nWe kept the pricing this flat on purpose. An eye clinic should choose software for the software. Whether you run a single-room practice or a two-room setup with screening camps on weekends, the plan you pick changes the billing rhythm and nothing else. Owners have enough arithmetic at the front desk already.\n\nPick a rhythm: monthly for trying the waters, quarterly for a season, yearly for the long run. Underneath, the workspace is the same complete one. That is the whole pricing story, and we are happy it is short."},{"id":"focuscasex-2-5-the-licensing-release","date":"2026-03-30","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 2.5: the licensing release","excerpt":"FocusCaseX 2.5 is out: activation codes, trusted devices, and the admin console with its Devices page. The licence is now part of the product, not a phone call.","tags":["release","licensing","update"],"body":"Version 2.5 is the release where the licence stopped being a conversation and became part of the product. Until this cycle, putting a new machine on FocusCaseX meant writing to us and waiting for a person. Now it means a code, an email, and a few minutes at the front desk.\n\nThree pieces ship together. Activation codes shaped FCX-XXXXXX, issued by the clinic owner and bound to the owner's email, one code per device. Trusted devices: a machine that activates is remembered, keeps working offline, and holds one of the seats under a device limit of two by default. And the admin console, whose Devices page shows every trusted machine, when it last checked in, and one click to revoke.\n\nThe offline-first promise carried through all of it. The first activation needs a connection once; after that, a trusted device keeps working through power cuts, weak networks, and camp days at the edge of town. Records stay on the device as plain files you can read and print, exactly as they always have.\n\nWhat did not change matters too. The workspace is the same one you know: clinical pages as files, capsules rendering live boards, extensions from the marketplace, themes and snippets as one-file customizations. Licensing wrapped around the product. It did not reshape it.\n\nExisting clinics get 2.5 through the usual fcx update, and the admin console opens with your devices already in view. New clinics meet activation as the first step of fcx setup. One code, one email, one device list. That is the whole story of this release."},{"id":"a-code-you-can-read-aloud-over-the-phone","date":"2026-03-27","type":"note","category":"Inside FCX","title":"A code you can read aloud over the phone","excerpt":"Activation codes get typed across a noisy front desk, so we shaped FCX-XXXXXX for exactly that: six characters, no confusing pairs, nothing that turns into a different character between being spoken and being typed. Read it aloud once, type it once, and the machine is trusted. The awkward letters never made the cut.","tags":["codes","design","clinics"],"body":""},{"id":"device-limits-in-plain-words","date":"2026-03-24","type":"note","category":"Feature","title":"Device limits, in plain words","excerpt":"Plain words first: every activation code carries a device limit, two by default, and the limit is a guardrail, not a meter. Nothing is metered, nothing is throttled, and the workspace on a trusted device is the complete one, offline included. A clinic that needs a third machine for a second room simply asks, and the limit grows with the clinic, not against it.","tags":["devices","licensing","limits"],"body":""},{"id":"revoking-a-device-calmly","date":"2026-03-18","type":"blog","category":"Security","title":"Revoking a device, calmly","excerpt":"A laptop retires, a licence moves on. What revoking a device does, what it never touches, and how a fresh code brings a new machine into the clinic.","tags":["security","devices","revoking"],"body":"There is a laptop in many clinics that has earned its retirement. The hinge is loose, the battery lasts through a cup of tea, and nobody has replaced it yet because it holds the workspace and it still opens. This week we want to show that replacing it takes minutes, not courage.\n\nHere is the whole procedure. Open the admin console and find the old machine on the Devices page. Revoke it: the device stops being trusted, its seat is freed, and nothing else happens to it. The records on that laptop do not move, do not change, and do not disappear. A revoke is a licence event, not a wipe.\n\nNext, issue a fresh code for the new machine. Every activation code is shaped FCX-XXXXXX, bound to the clinic owner's email, and each one carries a device limit, two by default. With the old seat freed, the new computer activates, the licence check confirms it is trusted, and the workspace opens on the new hardware.\n\nWhat revoke never touches deserves its own paragraph, because owners ask every time. It never touches patient records. It never reaches into the machine's files. An offline-first workspace keeps the records on the device, and a licence action stays a licence action. If the old laptop is heading out of the clinic for good, move its records from a backup to the new machine the way you already do; the licence has already moved on its own.\n\nA licence should move the way furniture moves in a clinic that is growing: calmly, in an afternoon, without a support ticket. Revoke, reissue, activate. The calm is the point."},{"id":"the-admin-console-who-is-signed-in-where","date":"2026-03-13","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"The admin console: who is signed in where","excerpt":"The admin console opens today. Every trusted device on one page: what each machine is, when it last checked in, and one click to free a slot.","tags":["admin","devices","console"],"body":"Ask an owner what they want to know about their licence and the answer is rarely technical. They want to see the machines. Which computer at the front desk is activated, whether the old laptop in the store room still holds a seat, and when each one was last alive. Today the admin console opens, and its first page answers exactly that. The page is called Devices.\n\nThe Devices page is a short, honest list. Each trusted device appears with a name you will recognise and the moment it last checked in. Nothing is buried three menus deep. You open the console, you look, and you know where the licence sits: which machines are trusted, which seats are in use, and how many remain under the device limit.\n\nOne click frees a slot. When a machine is being retired or a seat needs to move to new hardware, revoke it from the page and the seat is yours again. Revoking touches the licence and never the records; the patient files on that machine stay put, readable and printable as always. We will walk through a full retirement story later this week.\n\nWe built this page first because trust should be visible. An offline-first workspace keeps your records on your machines, so the list of machines is not admin trivia. It is the map of where the clinic actually lives, and owners told us they wanted that map without having to ask for it.\n\nThe console ships to every clinic with FocusCaseX 2.5 at the end of the month. Until then, the devices are already yours; now you can finally see them."},{"id":"one-code-one-email-one-device-list","date":"2026-03-08","type":"note","category":"Security","title":"One code, one email, one device list","excerpt":"The licensing model fits in three lines: one code activates one device, every code is bound to the clinic owner's email, and every trusted machine shows up on a device list that belongs to the owner. We do not hold your seats; you do. Three lines, no fine print.","tags":["security","licensing","ownership"],"body":""},{"id":"licences-that-travel-fcx-xxxxxx","date":"2026-03-03","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"Licences that travel: FCX-XXXXXX","excerpt":"Activation codes have arrived. One code per device, issued by the clinic owner, bound to one email. The licence moves when the clinic does.","tags":["licensing","activation","devices"],"body":"Every optometry clinic we know has one question that repeats with the furniture: when a new computer arrives and an old one retires, who is allowed to use the eye clinic software, and who decides. Until now that question ended in a phone call to us. From today it ends in your own hands. Activation codes are here, and they look like this: FCX-XXXXXX.\n\nThe shape is simple. One code activates one device. The clinic owner issues the code, and it is bound to the owner's email, the same address that opened the workspace. When the code is entered on a new machine, the licence check confirms it and that device is remembered as trusted. From then on the machine holds a seat on your clinic's device list, and the records on it stay exactly where an offline-first workspace keeps them: on the device, plain files you can read and print, online or not.\n\nOne choice was deliberate. The device list belongs to the clinic owner, not to us. You decide which machine holds a seat and when a laptop has served its time. If the front desk computer is replaced on a Sunday and the new one must be ready for Monday morning, you can do that yourself, in an evening, without waiting for anyone at CaseX Labs.\n\nLicensing had to fit the way this product already works, so it does. The first activation needs a connection once. After that, a trusted device keeps working through power cuts, weak broadband, and screening camps held where the network gives up. Your patient records never wait for a signal.\n\nThe code itself was shaped for real counters: six characters, chosen so they can be read aloud across a busy front desk without confusion. In the coming weeks we will open the admin console that shows every trusted device on one page, and we will talk about what revoking a device means, calmly. For now, the licence finally travels the way your clinic does: one code, one email, one device at a time."},{"id":"next-licences-that-travel-as-codes","date":"2026-02-27","type":"blog","category":"Roadmap","title":"Next: Licences That Travel as Codes","excerpt":"A first look at what comes next: licences as codes a clinic owner issues and revokes, devices that stay trusted, and no per-seat maze. Early days — nothing ships yet.","tags":["roadmap","licensing","clinics"],"body":"The past weeks were about agents; the next stretch is about the ground they stand on. Today, a first look at licensing — shared early and on purpose, because we would rather shape it with clinics watching than hand it over finished.\n\nThe idea is small enough to say in one sentence: licences that travel as codes. A clinic owner issues a code when a device needs one and revokes it when the device should stop. The licence lives with the owner, not with us. Handing a new machine its rights should feel like handing over a key you cut yourself, and taking it back should be just as direct.\n\nBeside the codes sits a simpler promise: devices that stay trusted. A machine that has been given a code keeps that trust until the owner says otherwise, so ordinary days never trip over permission prompts. Trust that is granted by a person and can be removed by the same person, with nothing in between.\n\nAnd no per-seat maze. Every clinic owner has met software where adding a chair to the room means a phone call and a small negotiation. We want the opposite: if the clinic grows from one desk to three, the owner issues another code and gets back to work. Simple to explain at the counter, simple to hand to whoever keeps the accounts.\n\nTo be clear about the ground: this is a first look, not a shipping note. The codes are on our bench, the wording is not settled, and none of it is in FocusCaseX today. What is settled is the direction — the owner holds the keys, devices earn trust and keep it, and the whole thing should be explainable in one breath at the front desk. If you run a clinic, tell us what a code should look like in your hands; we are listening while we build."},{"id":"fcx-and-the-agent-same-catalog-same-rules","date":"2026-02-22","type":"blog","category":"Automation","title":"fcx and the Agent: Same Catalog, Same Rules","excerpt":"The fcx command and a paired agent read the same catalog under the same rules: one workspace, many hands, one set of permissions. No side rooms, no second rulebook.","tags":["cli","mcp","automation","agents"],"body":"Some clinics run FocusCaseX entirely from the app; a few keep a terminal open beside it, where fcx does the steady work — setup on a new machine, a catalog check before the day, vitals before a camp, sync at close. When MCP and Automation arrived, we had a choice: give agents a private view of the clinic, or seat them at the same desk as the terminal. We seated them.\n\nSame catalog means what it says. What fcx catalog shows, the agent may read; what the agent reads, fcx can show. An extension installed by an agent, after its approval, is the same extension fcx install would have placed. A capsule drafted at midnight and approved at nine is on the page by nine, wherever you look for it.\n\nSame rules means scopes bind both hands. If an agent is read-only, it is read-only in every room, app or terminal; if a page is outside its scopes, no route reaches it. One set of permissions covers every pair of hands on the workspace, human or otherwise, and revoking a token ends the agent's reach the same moment.\n\nThis is also why there is no separate shelf for agent-made things. A capsule an agent drafted installs through the same one-tap path as anything else, and shows up in the same lists, beside the marketplace items it sits next to. Nothing lives in a side room that only the agent knows about.\n\nFor clinics, the takeaway is simple. Pairing an agent does not add a second system; it adds a pair of hands to the system you already have, governed by rules you already know. The terminal and the agent share a desk now. It was always one desk."},{"id":"what-clinics-automate-first","date":"2026-02-20","type":"note","category":"Community","title":"What Clinics Automate First","excerpt":"Among the clinics pairing agents, a pattern has appeared: the first automation a clinic approves is almost always the recall nudge, the second is the bill draft, and the last — often months later — is anything touching a prescription. We love the order. It says clinics read every diff before they trust it.","tags":["community","automation","clinics"],"body":""},{"id":"the-day-the-agent-filed-everything","date":"2026-02-17","type":"blog","category":"Automation","title":"The Day the Agent Filed Everything","excerpt":"A day with an agent at the desk: recalls set in the morning, reminders drafted by noon, the end-of-day report waiting at seven. Every step decided by the clinic, not the machine.","tags":["automation","agents","workflow"],"body":"Eight in the morning, shutters up, and the front desk opens the app to a small, tidy stack: the agent's overnight reading turned into drafts. Two recall nudges for patients due next week, worded gently. A reminder for tomorrow's first appointment, which was booked late on Saturday. Nothing has moved yet. Everything is waiting to be decided.\n\nAt eleven, between patients, the assistant approves the two recalls with a tap each and softens one line in the reminder, because the patient prefers a warmer opening. Then the agent asks, through the same queue, to note that a frame order arrived early. The diff is two lines long. Approved at the counter.\n\nThe afternoon holds a small screening camp, and camp days make mess: walk-ins, quick refractions, a rush at the store. The agent keeps pace by drafting — bill lines proposed as they happen, each one a pending request the desk approves between patients. The queue moves as fast as the counter does, which is the only speed that matters.\n\nAt seven, the day's last item: the end-of-day report, already drafted. What the desk saw, what the store moved, what remains open in the dues, who left with a follow-up. The owner reads it standing, taps approve, and the day is closed — filed, in the oldest sense of the word, by a person.\n\nCount the day and you count a handful of small decisions, each one a tap, none of them handed away. The agent did the gathering, the wording, and the remembering. The clinic did the deciding. That is how this day went, and how we intend every day to go."},{"id":"confirm-before-write-the-rule-we-kept","date":"2026-02-12","type":"note","category":"Security","title":"Confirm Before Write: The Rule We Kept","excerpt":"People ask why an agent cannot simply write and be trusted afterwards. Because trust is a workflow, not a checkbox: every write arrives as a pending request with a plain diff, and a human approves it in the app. It costs one tap and buys everything.","tags":["security","privacy","trust"],"body":""},{"id":"focuscasex-2-4-the-automation-release","date":"2026-02-07","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 2.4: The Automation Release","excerpt":"FocusCaseX 2.4 is out: MCP pairing with a six-digit code or QR, scopes, pending writes with readable diffs, and the three builders. Automation with your hand on the wheel.","tags":["release","mcp","automation"],"body":"Today we are marking a version rather than a single feature. FocusCaseX 2.4 gathers the past month of automation work into one release, and we call it the automation release for a plain reason: everything in it obeys the same rule — the agent proposes, the clinic decides.\n\nPairing leads the list. Settings now holds Pair an Agent: a six-digit code or a QR scan connects assistants like Claude, Cursor, or VS Code to your clinic. Scopes — read, write, build — decide what each agent may see and attempt, and a pairing can be revoked in a moment when it should end.\n\nThe heart of 2.4 is the pending request. Every write an agent attempts arrives in the app with a human-readable diff, and waits. Nothing changes in the records until someone with a thumb on the screen says yes. We did not build an exception into that rule, and we do not plan to.\n\nThe builders ship in the same release. Capsule Builder, Extension Builder, and WorkBench Builder each turn a plain description of the clinic into a draft add-on, reviewed in plain sight and installed the same one-tap way as any marketplace item. Your ideas and the marketplace's shelf now share one road.\n\nUnderneath, the old foundations hold. Records live on the device and the network stays optional, so automation works in clinics where the connection is moody. The fcx command reads the same catalog under the same rules, which means the terminal and the agent are two pairs of hands on one workspace, not two systems to babysit.\n\n2.4 is available as an update today. Pair an agent this week, grant one scope to start, and let the first pending request be a small one. The wheel stays in your hands; there is simply another pair of hands on it now."},{"id":"appointment-reminders-drafted-for-review","date":"2026-02-02","type":"blog","category":"Automation","title":"Appointment Reminders, Drafted for Review","excerpt":"The rule holds even for the most-wanted automation: the agent drafts appointment reminders, the clinic approves them. Here is what a week of approved reminders looks like.","tags":["reminders","automation","recalls"],"body":"Monday, nine in the morning. The weekend left behind a full Tuesday, and someone has to tell Tuesday's patients their times. Before the agent, this was a desk task that ate an hour; now it is a queue of drafts waiting for a thumb.\n\nThrough the week the agent keeps drafting: an appointment reminder for each slot, written plainly, in the language your desk actually uses. Each draft arrives in the app as a pending request — the same queue that holds every other write — with the message and the patient it is for. Nothing sends until it is approved.\n\nApproval is a small ritual that fits between patients. Read the wording, check the name and the time, tap. Most drafts pass unchanged. Some get a touch — a warmer opening for someone who has been coming for years, a shorter line for a student in a hurry — and the edit takes a moment because the draft is plain text you can actually read.\n\nBy Friday, the shape of the week is visible: reminders that went out on time, recalls set for next month, and a queue that never acted alone. The clinic's voice stayed the clinic's voice, because every message passed a human before it left the building.\n\nReminders were the automation clinics asked for first, and they are the reason we built the approval rule the way we did. The drafting is the agent's job; the decision is yours. That division has held in every pairing we have heard about, and we expect it to hold in yours."},{"id":"five-things-to-ask-an-agent-at-your-desk","date":"2026-01-30","type":"blog","category":"Workflow Tips","title":"Five Things to Ask an Agent at Your Desk","excerpt":"Five prompts that work from day one: tomorrow's recalls, this week's dues, the frames that moved, the bills still open, today's summary. Each arrives as a draft for approval.","tags":["automation","prompts","workflow"],"body":"An AI assistant is only as useful as the questions you think to ask. So here are five that work from the first evening, no setup beyond scopes, each answered as a draft that waits for your approval. They are phrased the way a desk would say them.\n\nAsk for tomorrow's recalls and the draft lists who is due, why, and a gentle message for each — approve them as a batch or one at a time. This one has saved more evenings than any other prompt we know.\n\nAsk what is still open in this week's dues and the draft names each balance in rupees, with the last visit beside it, ready to turn into a polite follow-up. Ask which frames moved this week and you get the store side: what sold, what sat, and what to reorder before the weekend crowd.\n\nAsk for the bills still open and the draft lines them up by age, so the close of day starts with a clear page instead of a puzzle. Ask for today's summary and the agent writes the day in a short paragraph — what the desk saw, what the store did, what waits for tomorrow.\n\nNotice the pattern across all five: the agent gathers and words, the clinic approves and sends. Nothing leaves, changes, or closes until a human taps. That is not a limitation we plan to remove; it is the design. Start with one prompt tonight, approve the draft, and see how the second one feels."},{"id":"the-end-of-day-report-drafted-for-you","date":"2026-01-27","type":"note","category":"Workflow Tips","title":"The End-of-Day Report, Drafted for You","excerpt":"Ask the agent for the day's numbers and it comes back with a draft end-of-day report: what the desk saw, what moved in the store, what is still open in the dues. The report runs itself every evening; the approval stays yours, one tap before you close the day.","tags":["automation","workflow","reports"],"body":""},{"id":"the-builders-capsule-extension-workbench","date":"2026-01-21","type":"blog","category":"Automation","title":"The Builders: Capsule, Extension, WorkBench","excerpt":"Three builders turn a plain description of your clinic into drafts: a capsule for the waiting room, a small extension, or a whole WorkBench. You review, then one tap installs.","tags":["builders","capsules","extensions","workbenches"],"body":"Most clinic owners do not talk in features; they talk in wishes. A screen for the waiting room that shows camp timings. A reminder card for dry-eye follow-ups. One setup where the desk, the exam room, and the store finally sit together. The builders are our answer to wishes: three of them, shipped with MCP and Automation, each turning a plain description of your clinic into a draft.\n\nThe Capsule Builder starts with the small screens. Describe the board you want, what it should show, how it should read, and a draft capsule comes back — a block you can paste onto a page, where it renders as a live board. You look it over, adjust the wording to sound like your desk, and install it. If it is not right, say so and it drafts again.\n\nThe Extension Builder handles the small features: a dues reminder card, a camp-day checklist, a compact view for the store. Extensions stay small on purpose — one focused thing that does one job well. Draft, review, install, and the thing runs beside everything else you already have.\n\nAnd when the wish is the whole clinic, the WorkBench Builder drafts a complete setup — front desk, exam room, and store laid out together — from a description of how your day actually runs. A WorkBench is the largest thing FocusCaseX installs, so its draft gets the longest look; but the path is the same honest one: read it, change what you want, install with one tap.\n\nAll three builders share one habit: they propose, you decide. Every draft is readable before it is installable, every install is one tap, and everything an agent drafts lives in the same places as marketplace items — no side room, no separate shelf. The marketplace stays the fast shelf for ready-made things; the builders make sure your own ideas can walk the same road."},{"id":"what-an-agent-may-read-and-what-it-must-ask-for","date":"2026-01-15","type":"blog","category":"Automation","title":"What an Agent May Read, and What It Must Ask For","excerpt":"Scopes in plain words: read decides what the agent sees, build lets it draft, and every write lands as a pending request with a diff you can actually read.","tags":["scopes","mcp","privacy"],"body":"The first question clinic owners ask about an AI assistant is not what it can do. It is what it can see. Fair question, and here is our answer in plain words: scopes. They are short labels you assign at pairing time, and they decide everything about the relationship.\n\nRead scopes are the eyes. You choose what the agent may open: the appointment book, the recall list, the dues page, the day's receipts. A front-desk agent does not need the clinical pages, so you do not grant them, and then it does not have them. If a page sits outside the scopes, it is simply not part of the agent's world.\n\nBuild scope is the making hand. With it, an agent can draft capsules for the waiting-room board, small extensions, even a whole WorkBench, working from your description of the clinic in ordinary language. Drafts, though, never installations: every add-on goes through the same review you would give anything from the marketplace, and installs with one tap of yours.\n\nWrite is the door with the latch. When an agent wants to change anything — move an appointment, edit a recall, add a line to a bill — the change does not happen on its own. It arrives in the app as a pending request, written out as a human-readable diff: this line would become that line. You read it the way you would read a note left by a colleague, then approve or decline.\n\nThat is the whole policy, and it fits in one sentence: the agent may look where you allow, make drafts where you permit, and touch nothing until you say so. Nothing applies on its own, ever. We think a rule you can say out loud at the front desk is a rule you can trust with the clinic."},{"id":"pair-an-agent-in-two-minutes","date":"2026-01-10","type":"note","category":"Automation","title":"Pair an Agent in Two Minutes","excerpt":"The whole pairing flow fits in a tea break. Open Settings, tap Pair an Agent, and a six-digit code appears on screen — or a QR if the assistant is on your phone. Paste the short line it gives you into your agent, send one test request, and approve it from the app — two minutes, and your clinic has an extra pair of hands that asks before it writes.","tags":["mcp","agents","setup"],"body":""},{"id":"agents-at-the-front-desk-mcp-arrives","date":"2026-01-06","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"Agents at the Front Desk: MCP Arrives","excerpt":"MCP and Automation is here: pair an AI assistant with your clinic using a six-digit code or a QR scan, give it scopes, and approve every write it proposes.","tags":["mcp","agents","automation","announcement"],"body":"It is 7:40 in the evening. The last patient left an hour ago, the register is closed, and the front desk is down to its quietest task: tomorrow's list. This is the hour we built FocusCaseX for, and today it gets a new kind of help. We are launching MCP and Automation — a way to bring an AI assistant into your clinic without handing over the keys.\n\nPairing takes about as long as making chai. Open Settings, choose Pair an Agent, and you get a six-digit code or a QR to scan. Your assistant — Claude, Cursor, VS Code, and others — takes the code, and the clinic appears on its side of the conversation. Like everything else here, the pairing lives on your device.\n\nFrom there, scopes decide the shape of the relationship. Read scopes say what the agent may see: today's appointments, the recall list, the dues page. Build scope lets it draft new things for the clinic. Write is where we drew the line: every write arrives as a pending request in the app, shown as a plain-language diff of exactly what would change, and nothing applies until a human taps approve.\n\nThat rule is not a courtesy; it is the design. An agent can read the schedule and draft tomorrow's reminders, but the reminders do not send themselves. It can propose a new capsule for the waiting-room screen, but the capsule installs the way everything does here — one tap, your tap. The assistant works; the clinic decides.\n\nYou stay in control the whole way through. End a pairing in a moment and access stops there. The evening hour does not disappear, but it changes: fewer errands, more judgement calls, and an extra pair of hands that never touches a record without asking. That is what we wanted automation to mean, and it is what ships today."},{"id":"2025-in-review-the-workspace-year","date":"2025-12-19","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"2025 in review: the workspace year","excerpt":"A quiet look back at the year the workspace grew a marketplace and a command line — what shipped, what held, and what 2026 is for.","tags":["year-in-review","workspace","cli"],"body":"December is for looking up from the bench. This was the year the FocusCaseX workspace grew its second story: above the clinic rooms where records live as plain files, we added a marketplace, and then, in October, a command line. It turns out a clinic wants both — a place to work and a way to reach it, even from the keyboard of the computer under the front desk.\n\nThe marketplace grew through the year: Themes that re-skin the whole workspace from one file, Templates for the notes clinics write most, Snippets that tweak a single file, Capsules that turn pasted blocks into live boards, Extensions that add one small feature each, and WorkBenches that set up an entire clinic in one install. In October the fcx CLI put all of it within reach of a terminal: fcx setup, fcx catalog, fcx vitals, fcx install. November brought fcx sync, so the terminal keeps its catalog current, and fcx update, so staying current is one habit.\n\nWhat held mattered as much as what shipped. The founding rule — records live on the device, the network is optional — carried through every release, including the CLI. Clinical pages stayed plain files you can read and print. And the scenes we build for kept being the same scenes: the front desk, the exam room, the camp hall with one bar of signal.\n\nAs for 2026: we have been spending December in the workshop, teaching computers to do the boring parts. The little experiments we wrote about earlier this month are the seed of something we mean to do properly — routine work that files itself, checked by people, not the other way around. That, and whatever you tell us the front desk needs next.\n\nThank you for a year of letters, camp mornings, and clinics that print things we never expected. The workspace is yours; we just keep the lights on. See you in January."},{"id":"a-year-of-clinic-notes-printed-and-bound","date":"2025-12-10","type":"blog","category":"Community","title":"A year of clinic notes, printed and bound","excerpt":"A clinic printed a full year of notes and bound them into an archive the front desk can reach. The plain-file habit, taken to its natural end.","tags":["community","plain-files","records"],"body":"Last week a photograph arrived from a clinic we have never written about, showing something we did not build on purpose: a shelf of bound volumes, spines labelled by month. At the close of their year, the clinic printed its notes and had them bound — a year of eye care, sitting where anyone at the front desk can reach it.\n\nWe did not build that shelf. What we built was the habit that made it possible: clinical pages in FocusCaseX are plain files, written to be read and printed, not trapped behind a format you need us to open. The clinic did not run an export project or wait on anyone. The files were already theirs; printing them was just reading, slowly.\n\nThere is a quiet argument in those volumes. Clinic records that live on your device, in plain files, can leave the screen whenever the clinic decides. They can be printed for a patient who cannot open anything. They can be bound, shelved, and handed to the person who takes over the practice someday. Ownership is not a feature we can ship; it is a stance we can only keep, and clinics keep proving it for us.\n\nThe detail that stayed with us: the volumes get consulted. When the machine is off and someone needs to check what was decided last spring, the shelf answers. Offline-first, taken to its most patient conclusion.\n\nWe are keeping the photograph pinned above the desk where we argue about features. It is the plain-file habit taken to its natural end, and it is the best year-end review of our own work we could ask for. If your clinic does something like this with its files, tell us. We are collecting these stories."},{"id":"telling-a-computer-to-do-the-boring-parts","date":"2025-12-03","type":"note","category":"Automation","title":"Telling a computer to do the boring parts","excerpt":"A workshop experiment, not a feature: a small script that sweeps the catalog each evening and files the day's paperwork where it belongs. It is crude, it is ours, and it is the seed of something we want to do properly next year. The boring parts are the first thing a computer should be asked to do.","tags":["automation","experiment","workshop"],"body":""},{"id":"offline-first-always-the-years-founding-rule","date":"2025-11-27","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Offline first, always: the year's founding rule","excerpt":"A year in, the founding rule holds: records live on the device and the network is optional. What offline-first means on a clinic day that refuses to go to plan.","tags":["offline-first","records","reliability"],"body":"Every clinic has one day a month that refuses to go to plan. The power dips in the afternoon, the router spends an hour blinking at the ceiling, and the waiting chairs stay full regardless. Days like that are why we wrote the founding rule of FocusCaseX before we wrote any feature: the workspace must work with the network off.\n\nA year of building later, the rule still holds, and it is worth saying plainly what it means. Your records live on the device, in front of you, readable as plain files. Nothing about a patient's history, a prescription, or a recall waits on a connection that may or may not exist. The network is welcome, but it is a guest, and guests do not hold the keys.\n\nOffline-first shows up in small, unglamorous places. The exam room opens the chart and the chart is there. The front desk prints the day's prescriptions without asking anyone's permission. A screening camp in a hall with one bar of signal still runs the whole day, because the whole day never needed the signal in the first place.\n\nThe CLI learned the same manners. fcx vitals tells you exactly what is reachable and what is not, and the catalog cache means fcx catalog still answers with the network off. Installing from the marketplace is the one errand that wants a connection; everything already on the machine stays yours. When the line comes back, fcx sync and fcx update pick up their threads.\n\nWe are not romantic about offline life; we are realistic about clinic life. Electricity and networks are the two things an eye clinic cannot schedule around, so our software does not try to. If this year had a single sentence, it would be the founding rule itself: the clinic keeps working, and the network does not get a vote."},{"id":"fcx-update-stay-current-without-thinking","date":"2025-11-20","type":"blog","category":"CLI","title":"fcx update: stay current without thinking","excerpt":"fcx update compares this build with the registry and prints the one line that brings it current. Knowing is the whole job; the habit is one command.","tags":["cli","update","maintenance"],"body":"The computer under the front desk is the patient nobody schedules. It runs for months, gets dust instead of check-ups, and only speaks up on the morning it matters. Its software ages the same way: not badly, just quietly, one release behind, then three. fcx update is our answer to that slow drift.\n\nThe command does one thing. It compares the build on this machine with the registry, and prints what it finds. If you are current, it says so and stops. If you are behind, it prints the one line that brings this build up to date, in plain words, and nothing moves until you run it.\n\nThat last part is deliberate. We did not want a command that rearranges your clinic while you watch something else. fcx update tells you the truth and hands you the exact line to run; you decide the moment. In a workspace where clinical pages are plain files you can read, the updater should be just as legible.\n\nIn clinics that have taken to the terminal, Friday now has a small ritual: fcx vitals for the machine, fcx sync for the catalog, fcx update for the build. Three commands, a column of green lines, and the week starts known. The whole ritual takes less time than the chai after it.\n\nStaying current should not be a project. It should be a habit with a name, and the name is fcx update. Run it when you think of it; let it tell you the truth; run the line it prints when you are ready. Small habits like that are how clinic records stay safe and Mondays stay boring, in the best sense of the word."},{"id":"the-laptop-behind-the-front-desk","date":"2025-11-14","type":"note","category":"Clinic Story","title":"The laptop behind the front desk","excerpt":"A clinic we know never opened a terminal in its life. Then the person who fixes the printer set up fcx on the laptop behind the front desk, and now the week ends with fcx sync, one command nobody else has to think about. The front desk runs smoother, and the printer still gets fixed.","tags":["clinic-story","front-desk","cli"],"body":""},{"id":"focuscasex-2-2-sync-comes-home","date":"2025-11-08","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 2.2: sync comes home","excerpt":"FocusCaseX 2.2 is out, and its headline is fcx sync: one command that keeps the terminal's catalog current, so app, site, and terminal read the same page.","tags":["release","sync","cli"],"body":"Today we are shipping FocusCaseX 2.2, and its headline is a word clinics have been using with us all year: sync. Specifically, fcx sync, the command that lets the terminal look after its own catalog the way the app always has.\n\nHere was the small gap. The CLI reads the same catalog as the app and the site, and fcx setup pulls it fresh on the first run. But a clinic machine does not stay at its first run. Weeks pass, the marketplace grows, and the terminal's copy of the catalog slowly became a photograph of an earlier day. Nobody complained, because nothing broke — but nobody wants yesterday's shelf, either.\n\nfcx sync closes that gap. One command, run whenever you like, and the terminal's catalog is current again — the same codes, kinds, and names the app and the site are showing right now. For the clinics that script their Fridays, sync slots neatly in front of fcx install: fresh catalog, then install, then close the laptop.\n\nThe rest of 2.2 is the quieter kind of work: care in the places you told us to look, and the offline-first stance untouched — records still live on the device, and the network is still optional. The CLI still runs on macOS, Linux, or Windows through WSL.\n\nThere is one more command on the bench for this month, about staying current without thinking about it. Until then, update to 2.2 when it suits the clinic, run fcx sync once, and every door — app, site, terminal — reads from the same page."},{"id":"fcx-install-the-marketplace-round-trip","date":"2025-11-03","type":"blog","category":"CLI","title":"fcx install: the marketplace round trip","excerpt":"fcx install takes a code or a name and does the whole marketplace round trip in one command — find, fetch, place — for clinics that script their Fridays.","tags":["cli","install","marketplace"],"body":"Friday evening, in clinics everywhere: the week is over, the chairs are up, and somebody is making sure Monday starts ready. In one clinic that means a checklist in a notebook. In a growing number it means a short script, and at the end of the script, a line that begins with fcx install.\n\nThe command takes a code or a name. By code is the exact way: type the code from fcx catalog and you get precisely that item. By name is the forgiving way: type roughly what it was called and let the catalog do the rest. Either way, fcx install makes the whole marketplace round trip — it finds the item, fetches it, and places it where the app will pick it up.\n\nEverything the marketplace offers can go this route: a Theme to re-skin the workspace, Templates for the notes you write most, Capsules that render live boards, Extensions that add one small feature, Snippets that tweak one file. And when the new machine for the exam room arrives, a WorkBench is a whole clinic setup in a single install — one command and the room is itself again.\n\nWhy bother doing this from a terminal at all. Because the CLI reads the same catalog the app and the site read, the item you install by code from the terminal is exactly the item your colleague sees in the app. A Friday script makes the routine repeatable: the same steps every week, no clicks to mis-click. The person who writes it once gets their Friday evenings back, or at least the part spent poking through menus.\n\nOne honest note: installing is an errand that wants a connection, since it visits the marketplace. That is the exception, not the rule — records live on the device, and the workspace works with the network off. Try fcx install this week, by name first. The round trip is shorter than you expect."},{"id":"fcx-vitals-a-heartbeat-for-your-install","date":"2025-10-28","type":"note","category":"CLI","title":"fcx vitals: a heartbeat for your install","excerpt":"fcx vitals prints one line per health check: the runtime, the workspace folder, the catalog cache, whether the marketplace is reachable. Green down the page means the machine is fine; anything amber tells you exactly which part needs a look. It is the closest thing a clinic computer has to a pulse.","tags":["cli","vitals","health-check"],"body":""},{"id":"fcx-catalog-the-marketplace-listed","date":"2025-10-22","type":"blog","category":"CLI","title":"fcx catalog: the marketplace, listed","excerpt":"fcx catalog prints the whole marketplace as a plain table — codes, kinds, names — and reads the same catalog the app and the site read, so nothing drifts.","tags":["cli","catalog","marketplace"],"body":"A question we hear at camps and clinics alike: what is actually in the marketplace. In the app you browse shelves; on the site you scroll pages. In a terminal, the answer is one command. fcx catalog prints the entire marketplace as a plain table and gets out of the way.\n\nThe table is deliberately boring. One row per item: a code, a kind, a name. Codes are short and stable, so they are easy to type later. Kinds tell you what a thing is — a Theme, a Template, a Capsule, an Extension, a Snippet, a WorkBench. Names are the human part, written the way an optometrist would say them. Nothing glows; it is a list, and lists are honest.\n\nThe part we care most about: fcx catalog reads the same catalog the app and the site read. One catalog, three doors. There is no second list for the clinic to keep current, and no version of the truth that only one door can see.\n\nIn practice this changes small moments. Someone in the exam room mentions a recall-letter Template they heard about; the person at the front desk types fcx catalog, finds it by name, and reads its code aloud across the room. The conversation stays in the room instead of turning into a hunt through menus.\n\nA good catalog is a map you can memorize. Next week we will show fcx vitals, the command that tells you the machine itself is healthy. Until then, try fcx catalog and see whether the marketplace starts to feel less like a store and more like a shelf you know."},{"id":"the-setup-wizard-question-by-question","date":"2025-10-16","type":"blog","category":"CLI","title":"The setup wizard, question by question","excerpt":"fcx setup asks four plain questions on the first run — workspace folder, catalog sync, sign-in, first WorkBench — and ends with a summary you can actually read.","tags":["cli","setup","wizard"],"body":"Every piece of software has a first ten minutes, and that is where most of our opinions live. So when we built fcx setup, we built it like a conversation at the front desk: one question at a time, each one in plain words, nothing assumed. The wizard runs once, on the first run, and by the end the clinic has a working terminal.\n\nIt opens with the only question that really matters: where should the workspace live. This is the folder where clinic records will sit, on this machine, readable as plain files. We ask because it is your decision to make, not ours, and because saying it out loud is the fastest way to understand what offline-first means here.\n\nNext comes the catalog sync. One short step, and the terminal reads the same catalog as the app and the site: every Theme, Template, Capsule, Extension, and WorkBench in the marketplace, listed and ready. From here on, what you see in the app and what you see in the terminal are the same list.\n\nThen a sign-in, so installs are attached to the right clinic. And last, the pleasant one: choose a first WorkBench. A WorkBench is a whole clinic setup in one install, so the wizard does not rush it — it describes each option in a sentence and lets you start with the one that matches your day.\n\nAt the end, a summary: four lines, plain words, the shape of the clinic in miniature. Answer something wrong, run the wizard again; nothing breaks, nothing hides. We wanted the first ten minutes to feel like being shown around by someone who works there. From what clinics tell us, it does."},{"id":"one-line-any-computer","date":"2025-10-11","type":"note","category":"CLI","title":"One line, any computer","excerpt":"Installing fcx is one line in a terminal. The command downloads itself, checks the machine it lands on, and is ready in under a minute — macOS, Linux, or Windows through WSL. The clinic's computer under the front desk counts as any computer.","tags":["cli","install","terminal"],"body":""},{"id":"fcx-the-clinic-gets-a-command-line","date":"2025-10-07","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"fcx: the clinic gets a command line","excerpt":"Today the FocusCaseX workspace gets a second door: fcx, a command line for the people who keep a computer under the front desk and like it that way.","tags":["cli","announcement","terminal"],"body":"There is a particular computer in almost every clinic we visit: a little older, a little dustier, parked under the front desk, switched on first and switched off last. The person who minds it is usually the same person who fixes the printer, knows where every cable goes, and would rather type two words than click through six screens. This post is for them. Today we are shipping fcx, the FocusCaseX command line.\n\nfcx is version one of a simple idea: everything the app and the site already know, the terminal should know too. The same catalog that powers the marketplace in the app now answers to fcx catalog, printed as a plain table of codes, kinds, and names. The same Themes, Templates, Capsules, Extensions, and WorkBenches the app installs with a click now answer to fcx install, by code or by name. Nothing new to learn twice — one catalog, two doors.\n\nGetting started is one line in a terminal, and fcx setup walks the first run in plain words: where the workspace folder lives, syncing the catalog, signing in, picking a first WorkBench. We built the wizard the way a good front-desk colleague explains things — no jargon, and a summary at the end.\n\nThe offline-first rule carries over untouched. Records live on the device, and the network is optional, including for the CLI. fcx runs on macOS, Linux, or Windows through WSL, and it speaks the way our clinical pages speak: plain text, plain tables, nothing you have to decode.\n\nThis is a beginning, not a finale. Over the coming weeks we will walk through the wizard, the catalog, vitals, and install, one post at a time. If you keep a computer under the front desk and like it that way, fcx was written for you. Tell us what you want it to do next — we are listening."},{"id":"one-quarter-of-the-marketplace-what-shipped","date":"2025-10-03","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"One quarter of the marketplace: what shipped","excerpt":"Three months of the marketplace: how the catalog grew from five Extensions to four shelves, what clinics reach for most, and what their requests ask us to build next.","tags":["marketplace","announcements","community"],"body":"The marketplace is a quarter old. In July it opened with five Extensions and a promise: small features, one tap in, one tap out. Since then the catalog has grown kinds, search, live demos, and whole shelves we did not plan in July. Before we point at the next quarter, it feels right to stand back and read the shelf.\n\nWhat the catalog looks like now: Extensions for the exam room and the counter, capsule packs for pages that need boards in a hurry, themes that match the mood of a room, and templates for the letters clinics write every week. Each shelf started from clinic mail rather than our roadmap. The vision-chart panel began as two letters in one week. The pediatric theme began as a story about a crying child. The camp-summary template began with a school that needed a report by Monday.\n\nWhat clinics reach for most is hard to rank and easy to describe: the features that sit where work already happens. Panels beside the refraction record. Boards at the front desk. References at the optical counter. Nothing on the shelf asks a clinic to change its day first; the Extensions that last are the ones that remove a few minutes from an hour.\n\nWhat the requests tell us: clinics want the shelf to reach further into their particular days. Camp teams ask for pieces built for the hall, not the office. Two-doctor clinics ask for counter features that respect an owner's caution. And almost everyone asks that new kinds keep the same shape: small, reversible, legible in a minute. That is the brief we are taking into the next quarter.\n\nTo every clinic that wrote, installed, removed and wrote again: the marketplace is yours in a way we did not fully anticipate. Keep the mail coming. The shelf is listening, and the next quarter is already half-shaped by your notes."},{"id":"five-extensions-for-a-busy-optical-counter","date":"2025-09-19","type":"blog","category":"Workflow Tips","title":"Five Extensions for a busy optical counter","excerpt":"For shops that sell as much as they examine: five Extensions and where each one earns its tap, from the lens-reference to the findings cheat-sheet.","tags":["extensions","optical-counter","workflow"],"body":"The optical counter is a different clinic from the exam room. Customers arrive with frames in mind, a budget in their head, and a prescription from somewhere else. The optometrist at the counter is part clinician and part shopkeeper, and the software has to keep up with a conversation that moves fast. Here are the five Extensions we see earning their tap at busy counters.\n\nFirst, the quick lens-reference: a one-glance table that answers the question customers ask most, whether a cylinder can move into the frame they are holding. It opens in a second and quietly ends a debate. Second, the bill-notes automation, which fills the notes line on common bills from the visit type, because nobody at a counter should type the same sentence twenty times a day.\n\nThird, the recall nudge. Spectacle habits run on years, not weeks, and the counter that remembers whose lenses are due tends to see those customers again. The nudge brings the due names onto the board the counter already looks at. Fourth, the vision-chart panel, repurposed for a quick check before a frame decision, so a hesitant customer can be settled in a minute without walking to the exam room.\n\nFifth, the findings cheat-sheet, which new counter staff keep open through their first weeks, until the abbreviations on a prescription stop being a foreign language. None of these five needs training. Each installs in one tap, sits where the work happens, and leaves without a fuss the day you decide you do not need it.\n\nIf your shop sells as much as it examines, start with the lens-reference and the bill-notes automation. They earn their tap within the first hour, and the other three will introduce themselves."},{"id":"focuscasex-1-9-the-marketplace-era","date":"2025-09-12","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 1.9: the marketplace era","excerpt":"FocusCaseX 1.9 is out: a deeper marketplace with smarter search and faster demos, calmer boards for pages clinics stare at all day, and the foundation the next year builds on.","tags":["release","marketplace","focuscasex"],"body":"Today we are shipping FocusCaseX 1.9, and the honest name for it is the marketplace release. This summer changed what FocusCaseX is: a workspace with a shelf. Clinics stopped merely asking us for features and began choosing them, and the product needed rooms for that habit. 1.9 is those rooms, finished properly.\n\nThe marketplace itself goes deeper. Kinds are tidier, search is smarter about the words clinicians actually type, live demos open faster, and the catalog stays one list across the app and the site. The shelves for Extensions, capsule packs, themes and templates sit in one place, so the marketplace reads like one store rather than four.\n\nBoards got calmer. The capsules that render appointments, vitals and recalls draw with less noise: gentler lines, clearer headings, spacing that lets a busy page breathe. The front desk stares at these boards all day, and calm is a feature. We adjusted spacing and emphasis across pages so an evening of backlog reads easier than it did in the spring.\n\nUnderneath, 1.9 is the foundation the next year builds on. The marketplace shape, one tap in and one tap out, is now how we think about every new thing. If a feature cannot be added and removed in a tap, it probably does not belong in FocusCaseX. We expect that rule to hold through everything we ship next.\n\nUpdate today and walk the shelf with fresh eyes. If a room in your clinic could use a small feature, it may already be there. If it is not, write to us. The shelf grows from your mail, and 1.9 is as much your release as ours."},{"id":"planning-for-world-sight-day","date":"2025-09-05","type":"note","category":"Eye Health","title":"Planning for World Sight Day","excerpt":"World Sight Day is October 9, and if your clinic is planning a camp or a screening day, the line matters more than the speeches. We keep a simple checklist: one page for the queue, a vitals board for the triage table, a camp-summary template for the school that hosted you, and a recall board so follow-ups do not live in anyone's pocket. The marketplace has all four pieces; the paperwork can disappear this year.","tags":["eye-health","camps","world-sight-day"],"body":""},{"id":"templates-marketplace-the-letters-clinicians-write","date":"2025-08-29","type":"blog","category":"Extensions","title":"Templates marketplace: the letters clinicians write","excerpt":"The template shelf is open: referrals, advice notes, camp summaries. One tap installs a pre-written letter, and the blanks stay blank until the visit fills them.","tags":["templates","marketplace","letters"],"body":"Ask a clinic what they write in a week and you get a short, unglamorous list: a referral letter to the eye hospital down the road, an advice note for a parent, a summary of a screening camp for the school that hosted it. The same letters, over and over, each one typed from scratch or hunted for in an old file. We think that repetition is a software problem.\n\nSo the template shelf is open on the marketplace. A template is a pre-written clinical note or letter with the clinic's blanks left open: names, findings, the plan. One tap installs it, and it appears where letters are written. Fill the blanks, print it or hand it over, done. The words that repeat stay; the words that change get typed.\n\nThe shelf opens with the letters we heard about most. A referral template that keeps findings in the order an eye surgeon wants to read them. An advice note for a parent whose child has moderate myopia, written in plain language. A camp summary that turns a day of screenings into the report a school asks for. Each one is one file, drafted with clinics and refined in use, and each can be removed as easily as it arrived.\n\nThere is a particular relief in watching a front desk use a template for the first time. The letter was always ten minutes of typing; now it is a minute of reading and checking. Nobody has to remember the phrasing for a lazy-eye explanation at the end of a long day, because the phrasing lives on the shelf.\n\nTemplates join Extensions, capsule packs and themes in the catalog. The marketplace is turning into a portrait of how eye care is practised: the panels, the boards, the colors, and now the words. If a letter your clinic repeats is missing, write to us. We will draft it with you and put it on the shelf."},{"id":"a-theme-for-the-pediatric-room","date":"2025-08-22","type":"blog","category":"Clinic Story","title":"A theme for the pediatric room","excerpt":"A clinic wanted a friendlier room for children; a theme with softer colors did it in one file. Clinics are places before they are screens.","tags":["themes","story","pediatrics"],"body":"A clinic in a crowded neighbourhood wrote to us about a problem that had nothing to do with refraction: children cried in the second room. The equipment was fine and the staff was gentle, but the room was white and sharp and felt like a place where things are measured. Could the workspace look softer, they asked, or does software not work that way?\n\nSoftware does work that way. A theme is one file that re-skins the whole workspace: the colors, the contrast, the way boards read across a room. Themes had just arrived in the marketplace, so we sat with the request and built one: calmer tones, softer edges where the eye rests, a palette that reads warm under clinic tube-lights instead of stark under white ones.\n\nThe clinic installed the theme in one tap before the morning queue, and removed it the same way when they wanted to compare. That is the point of the marketplace shape: a change this personal should be reversible in a second. They kept it. The staff say the room feels kinder, which is not a number we can chart, and the pediatric room now has a wall of crayon drawings to match.\n\nWhat we learned is bigger than the theme. Clinics are places before they are screens. An optometrist meets anxious parents and bored siblings before any eye is examined, and the workspace sits in the middle of that room with more influence over the mood than we ever gave it credit for. Eye clinic software is also software for the room eye care happens in.\n\nThe pediatric theme is on the marketplace now, alongside a small row of others. If your rooms have a mood you want the workspace to match, try one. One tap in, one tap out."},{"id":"marketplace-2-0-search-kinds-demos","date":"2025-08-16","type":"note","category":"Release","title":"Marketplace 2.0: search, kinds, demos","excerpt":"Marketplace 2.0 is live: real search that finds a lens reference even when you type two words, kind tabs that separate Extensions, capsules, themes and templates, and a live demo on every page so you can watch a panel move before it lands in your workspace. Browsing should feel like the counter, not a filing cabinet. Try the demos; they run right in the page.","tags":["marketplace","release","search"],"body":""},{"id":"one-catalog-three-doors","date":"2025-08-11","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"One catalog, three doors","excerpt":"The marketplace catalog is one list with three doors: inside the app, on the public site, and soon in a terminal. New items appear in all three at once.","tags":["marketplace","catalog","features"],"body":"The marketplace catalog now has three doors, and we want to be clear that it is one catalog, not three. Open FocusCaseX and browse the shelf from inside the app, next to your own work. Visit the public site and read the same items on a bigger screen, with longer descriptions for the person who researches eye clinic software after dinner. And soon there will be a third door: a terminal, for the clinics that like to type.\n\nWe built it this way because clinics kept telling us where they actually shop for features. An optometrist hears about a quick reference from a colleague at a camp and wants to look it up the same hour. The front desk wants to see what is new without leaving the appointments board. The owner, who signs off on everything, wants to read the whole list on the site with a cup of chai.\n\nOne list, kept current everywhere: when a new Extension appears, it appears in all three doors at once, and when a description changes, it changes everywhere. There is no version of the catalog that quietly falls behind, because the app door, the site door, and the terminal door all read from the same shelf.\n\nThe terminal is not here yet, and we will not rush it. But when it arrives it will read this same list, respect the same one-tap-in, one-tap-out rule, and change nothing about how the workspace behaves offline. Three doors, one catalog. The catalog is the product; the doors are only doors."},{"id":"capsule-packs-boards-you-can-hand-over","date":"2025-08-05","type":"blog","category":"Extensions","title":"Capsule packs: boards you can hand over","excerpt":"Capsule packs bring ready-made boards to the marketplace: appointments, vitals, recalls. Drop a pack into a page and the boards start working, no writing required.","tags":["capsules","marketplace","boards"],"body":"Capsules began as a paste trick. A receptionist pasted a short block into a page, and a live board appeared in its place: today's appointments, a vitals table, a recall list. Clinics took to it quickly, but not every clinic wanted to compose the block itself, so we asked what would happen if the boards came ready-made.\n\nThe answer is capsule packs, now on the marketplace. A pack is a set of ready-made boards: an appointments board with the columns clinics here actually use, a vitals board with the usual rows, a recall board that lifts the overdue names. You drop a pack into a page and the boards start working. No composing, no configuring, no syntax to learn.\n\nA pack is a starting point, not a cage. Because the boards are capsules underneath, you can paste your own beside them, rearrange the page, or remove the pack the same one-tap way as an Extension. Some clinics install the appointments board and never touch it again. Others take one look and start composing their own capsules the same afternoon, with the pack open as an example.\n\nWe watched a two-room clinic build a camp-day page this way: an appointments pack standing in as the camp queue, a vitals board for the triage table, a recall board for follow-ups, all on one page a volunteer could read at arm's length. The whole thing took an afternoon, most of which was spent deciding where the page should sit.\n\nCapsule packs sit on the shelf now, next to the Extensions. Same rule as ever: one tap in, one tap out, and the workspace stays yours."},{"id":"the-clinic-that-asked-for-a-vision-chart-panel","date":"2025-07-30","type":"note","category":"Community","title":"The clinic that asked for a vision-chart panel","excerpt":"Twice in one week, two different clinics wrote to us with the same request: put the vision chart where the refraction numbers are written. So we built the vision-chart panel, and in the marketplace's first days it became the Extension clinics reach for most. We keep a folder of clinic mail on our desk; the catalog is that folder turned into one-tap features.","tags":["community","extensions","requests"],"body":""},{"id":"the-first-five-extensions","date":"2025-07-24","type":"blog","category":"Extensions","title":"The first five Extensions","excerpt":"The opening shelf: a vision-chart panel, a recall nudge, a quick lens-reference, a bill-notes automation, and a findings cheat-sheet. Five small features, zero ceremony.","tags":["extensions","marketplace","panels"],"body":"A shelf is a promise about size. The marketplace opened last week with five Extensions, and every one of them obeys it: each is small, each does one thing, each can be gone by lunch if your clinic decides it does not fit, and the page will not miss it. We want to walk through them the way a colleague would, without ceremony.\n\nFirst, the vision-chart panel. It sits beside the refraction record and puts the chart you use every hour in the same view as the numbers you write. No more glancing at a wall chart and then back at the screen while a patient waits; the panel borrows a little space and returns a little time.\n\nSecond, the recall nudge. Each morning it reads your recall list and brings the names due this week onto the appointments board, where the front desk already looks. Nothing is sent and nothing is booked for you; it simply moves the reminder to the right surface. Third, the quick lens-reference: a one-glance table that answers, at the optical counter, whether that cylinder can move into the frame the customer is holding.\n\nFourth, the bill-notes automation, which fills the notes line on common bills from the visit type, so the front desk stops typing the same sentence all day. Fifth, the findings cheat-sheet: a quick reference of abbreviations and normal ranges that a newly hired optometrist can keep open through a first fortnight of exams.\n\nFive small features, zero ceremony. None of them changes how FocusCaseX works; each borrows a little space and gives a little time back. That is the shape we intend to hold as the shelf grows: if it needs a manual, it does not belong here."},{"id":"install-in-one-tap-remove-in-one-tap","date":"2025-07-19","type":"note","category":"Feature","title":"Install in one tap, remove in one tap","excerpt":"Our rule for the marketplace: adding a feature must never become a project. An Extension goes in with one tap and comes out with one tap, leaving the page exactly as it was, because a workspace you cannot undo is a workspace you stop experimenting with. We built the remove path first; clinics should feel free to try things on, and the workspace stays yours either way.","tags":["extensions","features","workspace"],"body":""},{"id":"the-marketplace-opens-extensions-arrive","date":"2025-07-15","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"The marketplace opens: Extensions arrive","excerpt":"The marketplace is open. Extensions are small features a clinic adds in one tap and removes just as easily: an extra panel, an automation, a quick reference.","tags":["marketplace","extensions","announcements"],"body":"This morning we opened the marketplace. Inside FocusCaseX there is now a shelf of Extensions: small features a clinic can add in one tap and remove just as easily. An extra panel on a page. An automation that fills a routine field. A quick reference that opens where an optometrist already looks. Nothing is welded on; everything is held gently, like a card in a sleeve.\n\nWe kept each Extension small on purpose. A vision chart panel beside the refraction record. A recall nudge that reads the follow-up list each morning. A lens reference that answers the question at the optical counter. If a feature takes more than a minute to understand, it is probably too big for this shelf.\n\nInstalling is one tap. Removing is also one tap, and the page returns to exactly what it was. That second sentence matters as much as the first. Clinics have told us for years that software grows heavier the longer you live with it, and we wanted the opposite: a workspace that carries only what you asked for, for as long as you want it.\n\nExtensions live the way everything in FocusCaseX lives: on your device, in your workspace, without needing the network to behave. The link can drop mid-morning and the panel still draws, the automation still runs, the reference still opens. Records stay on the device, and so does everything you build around them.\n\nThe shelf opens with a small, honest collection, and it will grow from clinic requests rather than our guesses. If there is a small thing your day is missing, write to us. One tap is all it should ever take to try."},{"id":"backups-you-can-hold-export-the-whole-clinic","date":"2025-07-11","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Backups you can hold: export the whole clinic","excerpt":"Export the whole workspace and hold your clinic in your hand: patient pages, prescriptions, reports — plain files you can read, kept on a drive you own.","tags":["backup","export","offline"],"body":"Ask an owner what they would save first if the clinic flooded, and the answer is always the same shape: the patients' files, the pending book, the work of years. In too many clinics that work lives inside one machine nobody dares to disturb — or, with other software, inside somebody else's computer entirely. This week we shipped the thing we have wanted to ship for a long time: export the whole workspace.\n\nThe export is the whole clinic, and it is readable. Patient pages, exam history, prescriptions, reports, the follow-up lists — plain files, openable on any computer, printable without us in the room. Nothing comes out scrambled into a format that needs a manual to decode. A clinic that can read its own data owns its own data.\n\nWhere should a copy live? Our suggestion is boring on purpose. One copy on a pen drive in the clinic drawer, carried home by the owner on the last Saturday of the month. One more on the computer at home, if there is one. Two places, neither of them ours, both of them yours.\n\nOffline-first was built for the exam room, and export completes the promise on the owner's side. Records live on your machine. Sharing crosses only the clinic's own network. And now the whole of it can sit in a drawer, safe from whatever the month brings. The network never held your clinic together, and it does not hold your backups either.\n\nThere is no ceremony to it. Open the menu, choose export, choose the drive, wait for the copy, put the drive somewhere a flood would not reach, and get back to the patients. A clinic's data should feel like the register at the desk: yours, in the building, in your hand."},{"id":"reading-glasses-or-refraction-a-note-for-front-desks","date":"2025-07-01","type":"blog","category":"Eye Health","title":"Reading glasses or refraction? A note for front desks","excerpt":"When a walk-in asking for reading glasses should be offered a full refraction instead — headaches, distance blur, and other signals the front desk can act on today.","tags":["refraction","advice","optometry"],"body":"The walk-in every front desk knows: a man in his fifties, briefcase in hand, no appointment, asking only for reading glasses. He is in a hurry and he knows exactly what he wants. The desk has a decision to make in the space of a breath, and many clinics make it differently every time.\n\nHere is the guide we share with front desks, in plain language. Reading glasses on their own are reasonable when the complaint is only small print, it began somewhere after forty, and nothing else has changed. Offer a full refraction when any of these show up: headaches by evening, blur at distance, one eye worse than the other, glasses that stopped helping, or diabetes anywhere in the picture.\n\nThe last one matters more than it sounds. A patient with sugars and unexamined vision is not having a spectacles conversation, and nobody at the desk should have to guess that from a poster on the wall. Say it simply: the full check takes a little longer today, and with your history it is the safer path. Most people take the safer path when it is offered without a lecture.\n\nThe workspace keeps the offer from becoming an argument. A full refraction is one page that moves: history at the top, readings beside the last pair, advice printed before the patient stands. The desk can propose the longer path without fearing it will swallow the afternoon.\n\nNobody expects the front desk to be a clinic. But the desk is where the day begins, and one kind question — did anything else change, or is it only the small print — catches the patients who came in asking for glasses and leave with something better: an answer, and a reason to come back."},{"id":"focuscasex-1-6-the-workspace-grows-up","date":"2025-06-24","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 1.6: the workspace grows up","excerpt":"Version 1.6 gathers the season: desk-ready reports, sharing over the clinic's own network, the exam flow on one page, and Snippets. A tool has become a workspace.","tags":["release","workspace","update"],"body":"Release 1.6 is not one feature; it is a season of work settling into place, and it is the version we would point a new clinic at. Reports the front desk runs on its own. The exam flow on one page. Sharing across the clinic's own network. Snippets for the small preferences every room has. Together they mark the line where a tool becomes something a team can run on.\n\nThe reports came first, in April, and they set the tone. One click, one page, printed at the desk: what moved, what is owed, what comes due. Month-end became a sheet instead of an evening. A workspace earns its keep at the front desk before it earns it anywhere else.\n\nThen the room connected to itself. LAN sharing let the front desk and the exam room look at the same files in the same moment, over the clinic's own network, with the internet nowhere in the picture. The slip-of-paper economy between the two rooms wound down on its own.\n\nThe exam flow pulled the chair-side clutter onto one page: history, refraction, findings, plan, advice, in the order the room actually moves. Assistants read it top to bottom. New staff stop needing a tour, because the page is the tour.\n\nSnippets arrived in June, and the appetite surprised us: bigger headings at the desk, a calmer palette in the exam room, one file each, here and gone. And the boards round out the release — the day's appointments, the pending work, the follow-ups coming due — visible without opening a single patient page, each one fed by the pages the team already fills.\n\nWhat turned a tool into a workspace, we think, is that a team can run on it: the desk without help, the exam room without clutter, the owner without asking twice. That is 1.6, and it is the version the rest of the year stands on. The workspace grows, and from here it grows where clinics point."},{"id":"next-a-marketplace-for-the-workspace","date":"2025-06-17","type":"note","category":"Roadmap","title":"Next: a marketplace for the workspace","excerpt":"A first look at what we are building next: a marketplace for the workspace, where a clinic can add a feature, a board, or a letter when it needs one — and remove it the day it no longer does. One tap in, one tap out, and nothing arrives that nobody asked for.","tags":["marketplace","roadmap"],"body":""},{"id":"two-branches-one-saturday","date":"2025-06-10","type":"blog","category":"Clinic Story","title":"Two branches, one Saturday","excerpt":"A two-room Saturday from the front desk: how the same workspace habits — one page per patient, a printed end-of-day sheet — hold when the clinic is split across town.","tags":["branches","habits","workflow"],"body":"The clinic runs two rooms across town, and Saturday belongs to both. The older branch opens at nine with the families who have been coming for years; the newer one, near the main road, takes the walk-ins. The owner starts the morning at one desk and ends it at the other, and the day has to make sense from both chairs.\n\nWhat surprised us, visiting on a Saturday, was how little is different between the rooms. Same page per patient: history at the top, refraction beside the last pair, advice printed before the patient stands. Same end-of-day report on the desk of each branch, one page each, waiting for the owner to read wherever the evening lands.\n\nEach branch keeps its own workspace on its own computer, and nothing has to travel between them for the Saturday to work. The registers are separate, the pending lists are separate, and the follow-ups due next week sit where the patients were seen. When the owner asks the newer branch what the day looked like, the answer is on paper, not in anyone's memory.\n\nThere is one thing the two rooms share, and it is not a cable: the routine. Print the day sheet before the shutters come down. Write advice as if the patient will not return for a year. Search the day, not the name, when a familiar face arrives. A clinic split across town still feels like one practice when the habits are the same in both chairs.\n\nWe did not build anything special for this story, and that is the point. Two rooms, one Saturday, and the ordinary workspace holding its shape in both. Practices are not always at one address, and the work should not assume otherwise."},{"id":"snippets-small-tweaks-one-file","date":"2025-06-03","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Snippets: small tweaks, one file","excerpt":"Snippets are here: a one-file interface tweak. Bigger headings for the front desk, a calmer palette for the exam room — pass the file around, turn it on, turn it off.","tags":["snippets","interface","customisation"],"body":"Some requests never reach a feature list because they are too small to be features. Make the headings bigger, the front desk told us — we read them across the room all day. Make the colours kinder to the eyes on long evenings, said the exam room. Both are fair asks. Neither deserves a settings maze.\n\nSo we built Snippets. A snippet is one file that tweaks how the workspace looks — nothing more, and deliberately nothing less. Open it, and the interface changes: larger headings for the desk, a calmer palette for the exam room, the small adjustments each corner of the clinic has been asking for. Remove the file, and everything returns to how it was.\n\nFiles travel the way clinics already share things — on a pen drive, across the room's own network, from one owner to a friend across town. There is no setup step to speak of; the file is the whole change. It is short on purpose, so you can read it before you use it, which is exactly the trust we want to keep.\n\nWe like what this does to the conversation with clinics. The smallest suggestions — the ones too minor for any roadmap — now have a home. When a room wants the interface to bend a little, the answer is a file, not a wait and not a maze. If the tweak turns out to be wrong for the room, deleting one file undoes everything.\n\nSmall is not a compromise. Most of what makes a workspace feel like yours is small — the size of a heading, the temper of the colours, the order of a morning. Snippets is our way of letting clinics decide those things for themselves."},{"id":"what-assistants-ask-us-most","date":"2025-05-27","type":"note","category":"Community","title":"What assistants ask us most","excerpt":"Every first week, assistants ask us the same three things: how to find yesterday's patients (search the day, not the name), which page prints the prescription (the one already open when the exam ends), and what to do when the internet is down (nothing — it was never needed). Those three answers usually settle the whole week.","tags":["community","training","assistants"],"body":""},{"id":"the-exam-flow-from-history-to-advice","date":"2025-05-21","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"The exam flow, from history to advice","excerpt":"History, refraction, findings, plan, advice — the full exam now lives on one page, arranged the way an exam room actually moves. Nothing to juggle, nothing to re-type.","tags":["exam","refraction","workflow"],"body":"An exam room has a natural order, and for a long time our pages ignored it. History lived here, refraction lived there, the plan went wherever there was space. The optometrist did the arranging, chair after chair, day after day. We sat through enough mornings to take the hint.\n\nThe exam flow is now one page that moves the way the room moves. History at the top: complaints, old spectacles, sugars, the last visit. Refraction next, with today's readings sitting beside the previous pair — refraction records that show the change at a glance, which is the only reason anyone keeps them. Findings, then the plan, then advice, in that order, because that is the order the chair actually follows.\n\nThe quiet benefit is what stops happening. No re-typing a power into the prescription, because the prescription reads from the same page. No hunting for the last visit's notes, because they sit on the same sheet. No advice drafted from memory at the end of a long morning, because advice closes the page.\n\nAssistants picked it up faster than anyone expected. The page reads top to bottom like the day itself, so a new assistant knows where an exam stands just by looking at it. A half-filled page means the patient is mid-exam; a page with advice printed means the chair is free and the desk can move the queue.\n\nNone of this is clever, and that is the compliment we want. An optometry EMR should arrange itself the way the room already thinks, and then get out of the way. Sometimes that is the whole feature."},{"id":"a-checklist-for-diabetic-eye-exam-days","date":"2025-05-15","type":"blog","category":"Eye Health","title":"A checklist for diabetic eye exam days","excerpt":"Diabetic eye days run on rhythm: what to ask in history, when to plan dilation, and how pre-written advice sends every patient home with clear instructions.","tags":["diabetes","checklist","clinic"],"body":"Once a month, a clinic we work with turns its Tuesday into a diabetic eye day. The register fills early, the same families return each time, and the exam room runs from nine to one without a gap. Days like this are won before the first patient sits down — by the checklist, not by the calendar.\n\nHistory first, and the same questions every time: when it was diagnosed, how the sugars have been running, whether blood pressure is part of the picture, what has changed since the last visit, and whether laser or injection has ever come up. Five answers, written once, and the rest of the exam already has a shape.\n\nThen the dilated plan, decided early. The fresh diagnoses, the patients with blurred readings, anyone who has not been dilated in a long while — they go to the end of the list with drops timed, so waiting becomes part of the plan instead of an argument at the desk. The queue forgives a planned wait; it does not forgive a surprise.\n\nAdvice is where diabetic days usually fall apart. The instructions are long, they repeat from patient to patient, and handwriting slows to a crawl by noon. The workspace carries pre-written advice for exactly this — sugars, follow-up gap, the warning signs worth acting on — and the optometrist adjusts a line or two before the print lands in the patient's hand. Nobody writes the same paragraph nine times.\n\nThe same checklist travels to screening camp halls, where the day is longer and the queue waits for no one. The takeaway is small: a diabetic eye day is a rhythm problem. History that repeats cleanly, dilation planned early, advice that prints itself. The clinic ends the day with the energy it started with, and next month's follow-up list already made."},{"id":"why-nothing-leaves-the-device","date":"2025-05-09","type":"note","category":"Inside FCX","title":"Why nothing leaves the device","excerpt":"Patient records live on the clinic's own computer — nothing is uploaded to be processed, summarised, or stored by anyone else, and nothing is sent anywhere in the first place. The workspace runs with the network off, because the network was never in the plan. If we cannot see your clinic's data, we cannot lose it — that is the whole stance.","tags":["privacy","offline","trust"],"body":""},{"id":"share-the-clinic-over-the-rooms-own-network","date":"2025-05-04","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Share the clinic over the room's own network","excerpt":"LAN sharing is here. When the clinic has its own network, the workspace opens on the front-desk machine too — same files, same moment, no internet involved.","tags":["network","sharing","offline"],"body":"The scene we kept hearing about: the optometrist finishes a refraction in the exam room, and the front desk is already asking what to bill. Until now the answer involved walking a few steps, or calling down the corridor, or writing the power on a slip of paper. This week, the slip of paper retired.\n\nLAN sharing is live. If your clinic already has its own network — the router in the corner that the whole room shares — the workspace now opens on the front-desk machine while the exam room keeps working on its own. Same files, same moment. Nothing travels across the internet; the sharing happens across the room, which is exactly the distance it needs to cover.\n\nWhat changes in practice is the quiet between rooms: the assistant sees the prescription the moment it is finished, so the billing question answers itself, and nobody re-types a power that was already written down. The exam room keeps its calm. The desk keeps its queue moving. The owner, passing through, can see the day building up without asking anyone.\n\nAnd when the network is off — the power cut, the router moved, the landlord drilling into a wall — nothing breaks. Records live on the machine they always lived on, and the workspace works the way it always worked. Sharing is a convenience layered on top, not a dependency underneath. That is what offline clinic software has to mean, or the word offline is decoration.\n\nOne network, one clinic, everyone looking at the same truth about the day. That is what we wanted the front desk and the exam room to feel like. Now they do."},{"id":"a-prescription-in-under-a-minute","date":"2025-04-28","type":"note","category":"Workflow Tips","title":"A prescription in under a minute","excerpt":"The fast path from exam to printed prescription comes down to two keystrokes most clinics skip: one carries the refraction you just finished into the prescription page, and the next sends it to the printer. Type the values once, glance at the print, hand it over — the patient reaches the optical counter before the chair is free again.","tags":["prescriptions","workflow","optometrist"],"body":""},{"id":"reports-the-front-desk-can-run","date":"2025-04-22","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Reports the front desk can run","excerpt":"End-of-day and month-end reports the front desk can run alone: what moved, what is owed, what comes due. Printed at the desk, not exported to a spreadsheet.","tags":["reports","clinic","workflow"],"body":"At ten past eight, the shutters are half down and the front desk is still lit. The assistant runs one report, waits for the printer, and carries a single page to the owner: today, on one sheet. What moved — patients seen, prescriptions printed, spectacles ordered at the optical counter. What is owed — the pending balances, name by name. What comes due — the follow-ups and recalls the clinic promised to call.\n\nThis is the report we hear about most, and it is the least dramatic thing in FocusCaseX. That is the point. Most eye clinic software treats the front desk as an afterthought; we wanted the desk to be the place the day makes sense. A report that needs a tutorial is a report nobody runs. Every one of these runs in one click and prints on one page, before the last patient reaches the door.\n\nThe same habit scales to the month. On the first working day, the front desk prints the month sheet and keeps it ready for the accountant's visit. Collections, pending amounts, the follow-ups that slipped past. The conversation that used to take an evening of tallying now takes one read-through over coffee.\n\nWe are stubborn about one detail: reports print, they do not export. Paper survives the power cut, the drawer, the owner's pocket. A file only one person can open helps nobody at eight in the evening, when the questions about the day are actually being asked.\n\nThat is the whole idea. The front desk does not need us, or anyone, in the room to answer a plain question about the day. The page already knows, and the printer is right there."},{"id":"focuscasex-1-3-faster-boards-calmer-evenings","date":"2025-04-18","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 1.3: faster boards, calmer evenings","excerpt":"Version 1.3 is out: boards that open quicker, a calmer end-of-day flow, and the small fixes clinics asked for by name.","tags":["release","updates","workflow","performance"],"body":"Evenings in a clinic have their own sound: chairs stacked, the counter winding down, the day's bills counted twice. It is also the hour when software tends to get in the way — the closing routine runs long, the boards feel slow, and every small irritation collected since morning comes due at once. Version 1.3 is our answer to that hour.\n\nThe boards are quicker. The patients board, the optics board, the recalls list — each opens snappier and moves the way the front desk thinks, with fewer beats between a name and its chart. On a crowded Saturday the difference reads as less waiting in the room, and everyone feels it even if nobody can name it.\n\nClosing time got its own calm. The end-of-day flow now walks in one straight line: the day's collection on a single view, open balances exactly where you expect them, tomorrow's due recalls a glance away. What used to be three notebooks and a mental checklist is now one screen and a short ritual the whole team knows.\n\nThe rest of 1.3 is a list of things clinics asked for by name: the prescription that printed one line short, a sort order that argued with the front desk, labels that wrapped at the wrong place, a button that sat just far enough away to be annoying. We keep a running note of every such request, and clearing it is a release of its own kind. Small fixes, honestly, are how software earns trust.\n\nFocusCaseX 1.3 is the same workspace you know, moving a little faster and ending the day a little quieter. Keep telling us what the evening feels like at your counter; that is the map we walk by."},{"id":"themes-one-file-a-new-coat-of-paint","date":"2025-03-28","type":"note","category":"Announcement","title":"Themes: one file, a new coat of paint","excerpt":"A preview of an idea taking shape in our workshop: one settings file that re-skins the whole workspace — colours, contrast, the feel of every board — without touching a single clinical page. A clinic that wants a calmer evening palette, or higher contrast for a bright counter, would change one file and watch the whole of FocusCaseX follow. Not shipped yet; we wanted you to see the direction first.","tags":["themes","design","preview"],"body":""},{"id":"recalls-that-bring-patients-back","date":"2025-03-21","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Recalls that bring patients back","excerpt":"Set a recall from any exam, read the due list by day, mark it done in one move. The recalls board is the quiet engine behind a full appointment book.","tags":["recalls","appointments","patients"],"body":"An appointment book never fills itself. Someone has to notice that a child seen last summer is due for a review, that a pair of eyes with rising power should be checked again, that the yearly exam season has come around. In most clinics that someone is memory, and memory leaks. The recalls board is our answer: a due list that does not forget.\n\nSetting a recall happens where the thought occurs, at the end of an exam. With the chart still open, set the follow-up: three months, six months, a year, or a date of your choosing, with a line about why. It lands on the recalls board without another screen, another login, or a note pinned to the desk calendar.\n\nThe board itself reads like a diary of the clinic's future. Open it in the morning and see who is due today, tomorrow, this week. The front desk works down the list, brings each name back into the appointment book, and marks it done in one move. Nothing gets chased across notebooks; the state of every follow-up lives on one board.\n\nA clinic working this way told us it felt less like software and more like staffing: as if someone had quietly taken over the follow-ups. Patients noticed too — being invited back on time reads as care, not marketing.\n\nSteady reviews are how eye health is actually protected, and a due list is how steady reviews happen. We suspect the recalls board will become the quietest, most valuable screen in the workspace — the engine behind a full book."},{"id":"a-full-screening-camp-one-laptop","date":"2025-03-14","type":"blog","category":"Clinic Story","title":"A full screening camp, one laptop","excerpt":"A weekend screening camp, registrations to printed prescriptions, run end to end on a single laptop in a hall with no network. This is the story and what it taught us.","tags":["screening-camps","offline","refraction","community"],"body":"The hall opened at eight on a Sunday morning: folding tables, a vision chart nailed to the wall, a line already forming under the fan. A team from a clinic we know had carried in one laptop and a small printer. No network reached that hall, and none was needed. By evening the camp had moved from registrations to quick refractions to printed prescriptions, entirely inside FocusCaseX.\n\nRegistration ran on the patients board, a row at a time: name, age, the street or village each person came from. Every registration became a chart file on the spot, so nothing from the morning had to be copied anywhere afterwards. The volunteers at the desk picked up the flow in minutes and the line kept moving through the hottest hours.\n\nRefractions were the fast middle of the day. Values went straight into each chart, today's numbers beside any earlier visit's, and the prescription printed at the table on the spot — folded, handed over, next name. Children who needed a closer look had recalls set before they left the hall, so the camp's promise did not end at the door.\n\nWhat the day proved is the principle we build on: records belong on the computer in the room, and the network is optional. Camps are where eye care meets people who may never visit a city clinic; software for that work has to run where the hall is, not where the signal happens to be.\n\nWe packed up with the printer smelling warm and a quiet kind of satisfaction. One laptop, one hall, a full camp — and every chart intact back at the clinic, ready for Monday morning."},{"id":"frames-and-stock-on-the-same-files","date":"2025-03-08","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Frames and stock on the same files","excerpt":"Inventory joins the workspace: what is in the counter, what moved, what a job consumed — and why stock reading the same files retires the spreadsheet copy.","tags":["inventory","frames","stock","optical"],"body":"Every clinic we spent time in had two truths about stock. One lived on the shelves and in the counter's memory. The other lived in a spreadsheet, updated on good intentions and usually a week behind. Both were believed, and neither matched. Inventory in FocusCaseX exists to end that arrangement.\n\nThe stock view reads the same files as the rest of the workspace. When a frame is sold, the optics order that sold it also settles the stock — the count drops the moment the bill is made. When a job consumes a pair of lenses, the movement is written where the job happened. Nothing leans on a staff member remembering to update a second sheet at closing time.\n\nWhat you get are the counter's questions answered where you stand: what is on the shelves right now, what moved this week, which models are thinning out before the weekend, what a particular job actually used. The frames board and the stock numbers are the same truth seen from two angles.\n\nThe deeper win is trust. When the counter says four pieces of a model and the shelves agree, the front desk stops rounding up in their head, reordering stops being guesswork, and the spreadsheet copy quietly loses its job. One set of files, one truth, and a stock count that is simply there when you look.\n\nA clinic that trusts its numbers orders calmer and sells better. That trust is the quiet product of stock and charts living in one place."},{"id":"templates-the-letters-clinics-actually-write","date":"2025-03-02","type":"note","category":"Inside FCX","title":"Templates: the letters clinics actually write","excerpt":"Today the first templates arrive: referral letters and advice letters, written the way clinics actually write them and kept as plain files. Open one, the patient's values fill in, adjust a line if you wish, print, done. A letter that took ten minutes of retyping now takes one, and more templates will follow the same rule — plain files, yours to keep.","tags":["templates","letters","referrals"],"body":""},{"id":"billing-that-matches-the-optical-shop","date":"2025-02-25","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Billing that matches the optical shop","excerpt":"Mixed orders of frames and consultation, part payments, and a bill that prints the same second it is made — billing shaped by how optical counters actually work.","tags":["billing","optical-counter","payments","workflow"],"body":"A real bill at an eye clinic rarely has one line. A customer buys a frame and two lenses, and the morning's consultation fee lands on the same slip. Someone leaves a deposit today and settles the rest on Friday when the spectacles are collected. Generic billing software treats all of this like a puzzle. We designed billing in FocusCaseX around the counter as it actually works.\n\nMixed orders come together naturally: consultation, frame, lenses, any service, all on one bill, each on its own line. Part payments are a first-class citizen rather than a workaround — take half now, see the balance printed on the bill, collect the remainder at collection. The bill always knows what is paid and what is open, and so does the front desk.\n\nSpeed is the other half of the design. The moment a bill is made, it prints — same second, plain paper, straight from the counter desk. A customer standing there should not wait for software to think; the receipt is in hand while the frames are being boxed, and the line keeps moving. On camp days and festival weekends, that pace is the difference between a calm counter and a crowded one.\n\nBecause billing reads the same workspace as everything else, the consultation line comes from the visit, the frame line comes from the optics order, and the patient's chart keeps the whole trail. At closing time, the day's collection adds up on one view instead of three notebooks, and an open balance from Tuesday is waiting on Wednesday morning.\n\nA bill is where clinical care and the counter finally meet on paper. It should take seconds, read clearly, and leave no argument behind."},{"id":"optics-and-dispensing-get-their-own-board","date":"2025-02-19","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Optics and dispensing get their own board","excerpt":"Frames, orders and jobs in progress now have a board of their own — the retail side of an eye clinic, reading the very same patient files as the exam room.","tags":["optics","dispensing","frames","optometry"],"body":"Walk from the exam room to the optical counter and the mood changes. The chair side is clinical and quiet; the counter is shelves, delivery dates and a customer asking whether the frame she liked last week has arrived. Most clinical software treats the second room as an afterthought. We gave it a board of its own.\n\nThe optics board keeps the dispensing side of the house in one view: the frames on display, the orders taken, and every job in progress between choice and collection. An order carries the prescription it came from, the frame it was sold with, the lens type, the stage it has reached, and the day it should be ready. Open the board in the morning and the counter's day is already laid out.\n\nThe part we care about most: the optics board reads the same patient files as the exam side. When a customer walks in to collect, the counter opens the chart — the same plain chart the optometrist wrote — and the prescription, the order and the history are together in one place. No retold details, no paper slip carried between rooms.\n\nA clinic that started using the board described the quiet benefit: jobs stopped getting lost between the exam room and the counter, because both rooms were finally looking at the same thing. The optometrist can see which prescriptions became orders; the counter can see which orders are waiting on lenses. One workspace, both halves of the clinic.\n\nDispensing is not a side business of an eye clinic — it is half the promise a clinic makes. It deserves a board that treats it that way."},{"id":"why-refraction-history-sits-side-by-side","date":"2025-02-14","type":"note","category":"Eye Health","title":"Why refraction history sits side by side","excerpt":"A small design decision carries real clinical weight: today's refraction opens beside last visit's, on the same screen, in the same glance. A shift of half a dioptre is exactly the thing a busy day hides and a side-by-side view shows. In this workspace a change in power is seen, not remembered — which is how myopia drift and early lens changes deserve to be caught.","tags":["refraction","eye-health","clinical"],"body":""},{"id":"prescriptions-without-retyping","date":"2025-02-10","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Prescriptions without retyping","excerpt":"Refraction values carry straight into the prescription, the page prints clean, and the history stays with the patient. No second typing, no second place to err.","tags":["prescriptions","refraction","printing","workflow"],"body":"Six numbers decide a pair of spectacles: sphere and cylinder for each eye, the axis, the addition. In plenty of clinics those numbers are still typed twice — once into the worksheet, once onto the prescription pad — and every second typing is a second chance to transpose a digit. We wanted that chance gone from the room.\n\nIn FocusCaseX the prescription is born from the refraction. Finish the exam, open the prescription page, and the powers are already sitting in it, carried over from the chart you just wrote. Adjust a value in the refraction and the prescription follows. What you examined is what you prescribe, with no human copyist in between and no second place to slip.\n\nThe printed page matters as much as the flow. A prescription gets folded into a handbag, carried to an optical shop, compared against an older pair at home. So it prints clean on plain paper: the clinic's name at the top, the patient's details, the powers set out clearly, the advice, the date, and a line for your signature. Nothing cropped, nothing squeezed into a strange grid.\n\nAnd because every prescription lives inside the chart file, the history stays with the patient. When someone returns in March saying the new pair feels wrong, you open the chart and see every slip ever issued, each one beside the refraction that produced it. The comparison takes seconds instead of an archive hunt through older files.\n\nA prescription is the one page of your software most patients will ever hold. It should be the easiest thing you make all day."},{"id":"search-that-finds-the-chart-not-the-folder","date":"2025-02-05","type":"note","category":"Workflow Tips","title":"Search that finds the chart, not the folder","excerpt":"Type three letters and you are on the patient's chart — no folder digging, no remembering which visit held it. Search in FocusCaseX reads the charts themselves, not a separate list kept somewhere else, so it cannot drift out of step with what is actually written. The chart you find is the chart that exists.","tags":["search","workflow","charts"],"body":""},{"id":"every-patient-one-chart-file","date":"2025-02-01","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Every patient, one chart file","excerpt":"The patients board gives every person one chart file that holds the whole history: refractions, prescriptions, notes and recalls, in plain pages you can read and print.","tags":["patients","chart-file","records","optometry-emr"],"body":"Where does a patient actually live in your software? In many systems the answer is a maze: one screen for registration, another for visits, a third for the spectacle power, and an export button somewhere if you ever want the whole story. We thought a person deserves better than a maze.\n\nSo the patients board in FocusCaseX is one list. Every person who has ever walked in sits in the same place — regulars from the neighbourhood, first-time walk-ins, the schoolchildren met at a camp. A few letters of a name bring the row up; one click opens the chart. The front desk can run it at the speed of conversation.\n\nThe chart itself is a plain file, and that is the point. Open it and the story reads from top to bottom: the first refraction, the change a year later, the prescriptions printed along the way, the advice given, the recall set for next season. Nothing waits in a tab you have to guess at. You can read it at the desk, print it for the patient, and it stays legible years from now.\n\nAn optometrist in a busy two-chair practice described what this changed in her mornings: she opens the day's appointments, reads each chart before the patient sits down, and by the time the trial frame is in place she already knows last visit's power and the complaint that brought them back. The exam starts ahead instead of starting late.\n\nA chart file is a small promise with a long reach: the clinic's history belongs to the clinic, in a form a human can simply open. That promise is shaping everything we build on the patients board next."},{"id":"focuscasex-1-0-the-workspace-opens","date":"2025-01-28","type":"blog","category":"Release","title":"FocusCaseX 1.0: the workspace opens","excerpt":"FocusCaseX 1.0 is out: patients, refractions, prescriptions, appointments and recalls in one offline-first workspace for eye clinics. Here is what day one looks like.","tags":["release","eye-clinic-software","optometry-emr","offline"],"body":"Most software asks a clinic to bend around it. We spent a long year sitting in optometry clinics and camp halls before writing a line of this one, and we kept noticing the same rhythm: register, examine, refract, prescribe, dispense, call back. FocusCaseX is built to follow that rhythm rather than rewrite it. Today, version 1.0 opens the workspace.\n\nDay one gives a clinic the whole core. A patients board where every person is one row and one chart file. Refraction entries that sit beside the previous visit's numbers. Prescriptions that pull their values straight from the refraction and print clean on plain paper. An appointments view for the chair side, and a recalls board so follow-ups stop depending on anyone's memory.\n\nTwo choices run under all of it. Every clinical page is a plain file — readable, printable, and yours to keep. And every record lives on your own computer, in the room where the work happens. A dead connection never stops an exam; the network is optional and the workspace is not.\n\nThe first install is an afternoon, not a week. Put FocusCaseX on the computer at the front desk, enter the clinic's name and timings, and the patients board is ready before the first appointment walks in. There is no training marathon and no consultant on retainer. An optometrist who set it up told us the strangest part was how little there was to learn.\n\nThis is the opening, not the finished building. The boards that run the exam room, the counter and the follow-up list are here now; the rest will grow the way clinics grow, one real need at a time. If you run an eye clinic and want software that behaves like a good assistant instead of a second job, the door is open. FocusCaseX 1.0 is waiting inside."},{"id":"one-month-in-what-changed","date":"2025-01-24","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"One month in: what changed","excerpt":"A month of building in the open: patient charts, refraction capture, the day board and recalls are real — plus the short list of what comes next.","tags":["progress","roadmap","announcement"],"body":"One month ago the first post on this page was a few hundred words about paper files and a promise to build in the open. Since then the prototype has grown from an idea with one patient page into something clinics have started asking to keep. Here is what changed, plainly.\n\nPatient charts are real now. A page exists the moment a patient is registered, reads like a document and prints like one. The refraction and exam capture flow sits inside that page, so the numbers an optometrist measures become part of the file in the same visit, not later and not somewhere else.\n\nThe appointments board arrived this month and gave the front desk its first screen worth keeping open: the day at a glance, read straight from the patient files, with no separate calendar to argue with. Basic recalls came along with it, set in three taps from the refraction flow, quietly stocking the clinic's future afternoons with return visits.\n\nThe idea we are chasing next is the one we wrote about at the end of December: capsules, small pasted blocks that could render live boards right inside a page. Nothing ships yet. We are also carrying a short list from clinics, billing that reads the prescription it bills and a stock tray that remembers the frames, and those conversations decide the order we build in.\n\nWhat we did not expect was how much of this month was shaped by reading rather than building: sitting at front desks, watching how a prescription travels from exam room to optical counter, listening to what the assistant reads aloud. Every feature above came out of one of those afternoons.\n\nThe next month already has a shape: sharper boards, stronger recalls and the first honest attempt at a capsule. We will keep writing these notes as things land. If the workspace keeps earning its place on the desk beside the register it means to replace, the month after this one will be worth reporting too."},{"id":"the-three-tap-recall","date":"2025-01-16","type":"note","category":"Workflow Tips","title":"The three-tap recall","excerpt":"Setting a recall now takes three taps from the refraction flow: choose the interval, confirm the reason, save. The recall stays with the patient and appears when a quiet afternoon needs filling. A well-fed recall list is how an optometry clinic keeps February busy without dialling a single stranger.","tags":["recalls","workflow","tips"],"body":""},{"id":"the-appointments-board-first-light","date":"2025-01-11","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"The appointments board, first light","excerpt":"The appointments board is here: the day at a glance, read straight from the patient files the clinical pages already use. No double entry, no separate calendar.","tags":["appointments","boards","workflow"],"body":"Our January notes promised a day board, and this week it stopped being a promise. The appointments board is live in the workspace: the whole day in one view, from the first booking to the last walk-in, on the screen the front desk keeps open between phone calls.\n\nThe part we care about most is invisible. The board does not keep its own copy of anything. It reads the same patient files the clinical pages use, so an appointment is a line in a patient's file wearing its daytime clothes. Book someone, and the board shows it. Add a walk-in, and the board makes room. Nothing is typed twice, because there is only one place the day is written down.\n\nThe first clinic we showed it to ran a small test we now repeat every morning ourselves. Book a follow-up from the exam flow, then look at the board: the slot is there, with the patient's name spelled the way their file spells it. No separate calendar to reconcile, no evening spent checking whether the register and the screen disagree.\n\nThere is a particular relief on the reception side that we did not fully anticipate. The person at the desk answers the phone with the answer already on screen: yes, the doctor has a slot at that hour, and yes, we can see the previous refraction before you arrive. Fewer promises made on hope, fewer apologies at the door.\n\nNext on this board, making the walk-in flow faster and giving recalls a proper queue to sit in. For now the day has a home, and it is the same home the patient files live in. That felt worth a quiet celebration, and then, immediately, back to work."},{"id":"what-clinics-asked-for-in-january","date":"2025-01-06","type":"blog","category":"Roadmap","title":"What clinics asked for in January","excerpt":"Appointments, recalls, billing, stock — the four boards clinics kept asking for, and why all of them should read from the same patient files.","tags":["roadmap","boards","clinics"],"body":"Ask clinics what they want and the answers stop being polite very quickly, which is exactly what we hoped for. Over the last few weeks we wrote down every request that arrived during conversations, demos on borrowed laptops and one long phone call after closing time. Four asks kept repeating.\n\nThe first is appointments, and it is the loudest. Clinics want the day on one board when the doors open: who is booked, who walked in, who is running late, without opening a patient page to find out. We agree, and that is the piece we are building right now.\n\nThe second is recalls with more strength behind them. The basic recall exists and clinics already use it, but they want the list itself to be a working surface, a queue of patients due back, grouped by month, ready to be worked through on a slow afternoon. Third is billing that reads the same files the clinical pages do, so a spectacles order at the optical counter never has to be typed twice. Fourth is stock, the tray of frames and lenses that currently lives in somebody's memory.\n\nThe common thread in all four is the one we started with: the patient's file should be the source, and every board should be a different way of reading it. A recall should grow out of the refraction that prompted it. A bill should know the prescription it belongs to. Double entry is not a workflow, it is a leak.\n\nSo that is the shape of the next few months: appointments first, then the recall queue, then the rest in the order clinics ask loudest. We will keep writing here as each one lands. If your clinic has a fifth ask that did not make this list, we genuinely want to hear it."},{"id":"capsules-the-idea-that-sticks","date":"2024-12-27","type":"note","category":"Announcement","title":"Capsules: the idea that sticks","excerpt":"As the year ends, here is the idea we cannot put down: paste a small block into any page and a live board appears right there — appointments, vitals, recalls, whatever that page needs. We are calling these capsules. Nothing ships yet; the thought we carry into the new year is that a clinical page should be able to hold its own tools.","tags":["capsules","idea","design"],"body":""},{"id":"one-week-in-a-clinic-with-no-internet","date":"2024-12-20","type":"blog","category":"Clinic Story","title":"One week in a clinic with no internet","excerpt":"We ran the prototype for a week where the internet drops daily and nothing broke, because the records live on the device. Offline-first, earned early.","tags":["offline","clinics","reliability"],"body":"Internet at our test clinic gives up most afternoons, limps back for a while, and disappears entirely whenever it rains. We knew this before we started and chose the clinic partly because of it. If a workspace for eye care cannot survive that street, it does not deserve the clinics on it.\n\nFor one week in December the prototype ran there every day. Mornings were ordinary: registering walk-ins, capturing refraction on the exam flow, checking the small day list we had set up. Then some time after lunch the network would drop, and nothing at the desk changed at all. Pages opened the same way. Values saved the same way.\n\nThe reason is a decision we made before writing the first screen: patient records live on the device. The machine in the clinic holds its own history and the network is an option, not a dependency. When the connection came back, nothing needed rescuing. There was no queue of failures waiting at four in the afternoon, because nothing was waiting on the network in the first place.\n\nWhat surprised us was the mood in the room. Staff stopped mentioning the internet by the second day. The front desk stopped preparing an apology for slow systems that never arrived. The optometrist worked through the afternoon as if the missing internet were somebody else's problem, which, for clinical work, it now was.\n\nWe say offline-first because it describes where the records live, not where we wish the internet were. After that week the phrase stopped being a line on a feature list and became something we had watched hold up a working day. We want every clinic that works through a power cut to feel exactly what that room felt."},{"id":"first-feedback-from-a-working-clinic","date":"2024-12-16","type":"note","category":"Community","title":"First feedback from a working clinic","excerpt":"Listening to the first clinician try the prototype taught us more than any plan ever could. After a few seconds at our opening screen came the line that became our measuring stick: my assistant has to be able to read this, not me. Within the week every label was rewritten in words a front desk already uses, and we have repeated that exercise many times since.","tags":["feedback","community","design"],"body":""},{"id":"pages-that-print-like-paper","date":"2024-12-11","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"Pages that print like paper","excerpt":"Every clinical page reads like a document and prints like one — prescriptions, notes, bills — so a clinic keeps its paper habits without a paper system.","tags":["printing","prescriptions","paper"],"body":"Print is the one interface every clinic already trusts, so we gave it a place in the design from the first week. Every clinical page in the workspace reads like a document and prints like one: the prescription the optometrist just finished, the notes from a follow-up, the bill for a pair of spectacles from the optical counter.\n\nThe reason is practical, not nostalgic. Prescriptions travel. They go home with patients, they go to the optical shop downstairs, they go into a camp hall where someone wants a slip to carry away. Notes and bills get filed, pinned and compared. If the software cannot produce a page that fits these habits, the clinic ends up keeping two systems, one for the computer and one for the truth. We have seen that happen and it is worse than paper alone.\n\nSo the pages are written to be read by people first. Plain headings, values spelled the way an optometrist says them, nothing that needs a manual to interpret. The print output is the same page sized for paper, because a page that looks different when printed is a page nobody will trust twice.\n\nThere is a quieter point underneath. A clinic should be able to keep its paper habits without keeping a paper system. Print the prescription because the patient expects to hold one. Print the day's notes because the senior doctor likes to read them after closing. The files still live on the machine, searchable and safe from the damp drawer, and paper becomes an output instead of the only copy.\n\nThe next time the power flickers mid-afternoon, the clinic will not notice much. The pages are already on the device and the printer does not care whether the internet is up. That is the kind of ordinary resilience we are aiming for, and printing turns out to be a bigger part of it than we expected."},{"id":"refraction-typed-once","date":"2024-12-06","type":"note","category":"Inside FCX","title":"Refraction, typed once","excerpt":"In the first prototype, refraction entry is a single block: sphere, cylinder and axis for each eye, plus the add and the working distance. The rule we set ourselves was simple — a value typed once should follow the patient to every later visit, so the optometrist reads history instead of re-asking it. Nothing about refraction should need to be memorised, only confirmed.","tags":["refraction","prototype","design"],"body":""},{"id":"the-first-patient-chart-saved","date":"2024-12-02","type":"blog","category":"Feature","title":"The first patient chart, saved","excerpt":"Register a patient and the chart is already there, dated and waiting — no new-patient form, no folder hunt. What pages as plain files means in practice.","tags":["charts","files","workflow"],"body":"It happened on an ordinary Tuesday, which is how the good moments usually arrive. We registered a test patient in the prototype and braced for the usual next step, a form with fields and a save button at the bottom. Instead the chart was simply there: named for the patient, dated, waiting under the refraction section for the first numbers.\n\nThat is now the rule the workspace follows. A patient page comes into existence the moment the patient does. There is no new-patient form to complete before work can begin and no folder to hunt for when the same person walks in next month. Registration is one step, and the chart is the step after it, which is really no step at all.\n\nThe pages themselves are plain files. Anyone at the front desk can open a chart and read it top to bottom, the demographics line, the refraction history, the notes from each visit, the same way they would read the paper file it will one day sit beside. Nothing waits behind a dashboard. What the clinician sees is what the page contains.\n\nThis sounds small and it changes the day completely. A walk-in can be seated and seen without a data-entry pause at the desk. A locum optometrist can find a returning patient's history in seconds by reading rather than by learning the software. And when something goes wrong, a page that reads like a document is a page a human can fix.\n\nWe kept that first chart on the test machine and open it sometimes. It holds one line of invented refraction values and a print of itself from the same afternoon. Most teams would have deleted such a thing by now. We decided ours could stay, as a reminder of the shape we want every page to keep."},{"id":"how-focuscasex-got-its-name","date":"2024-11-27","type":"blog","category":"Inside FCX","title":"How FocusCaseX got its name","excerpt":"Focus for the exam, case for the file the patient really is, X for everything a clinic does beyond the exam chair — the story behind our name.","tags":["naming","brand","focuscasex"],"body":"We went through a whole notebook of names before the right one showed up, and most of them were bad in forgettable ways. Some sounded like lab equipment. Some tried too hard to say technology. We wanted something a receptionist could say over the phone without slowing down.\n\nFocus came first, for the obvious reason and a quieter one. The obvious one is the exam itself: refraction is an exercise in focus, and an optometrist spends the day helping eyes find it. The quieter one is what we want the software to do for the clinic — hold attention on the patient in the chair instead of on the screen.\n\nCase is the part that matters most to us. In Indian clinics the patient still arrives as a case file, a folder with a name and a history folded inside. Our patient pages are plain files for exactly that reason: openable, readable, printable, yours. The case file is the product. Everything else arranges itself around it.\n\nThe X stands for everything that happens beyond the exam chair, which is where clinics actually lose their day: the register at the front desk, the optical counter, the recall card that never got written, the screening camp that runs on paper slips. An eye clinic is not one room. It is several rooms that have to agree with each other.\n\nPut together it reads as FocusCaseX, and we shortened nothing because every part is doing work. CaseX Labs is the name of our studio, and the workspace carries the same logic. A name that has to survive being said a hundred times a day should also mean something when you stop and look at it."},{"id":"two-screens-and-a-decision","date":"2024-11-22","type":"note","category":"Inside FCX","title":"Two screens and a decision","excerpt":"Back in November the whole prototype was one laptop and a borrowed monitor, and we had a choice about what the second screen was for. We decided it should belong to the patient page, not to menus — a clinician mid-refraction should never have to hunt for a value they just typed. Every screen since has kept that rule.","tags":["prototype","design","attention"],"body":""},{"id":"it-starts-with-a-paper-file","date":"2024-11-18","type":"blog","category":"Announcement","title":"It starts with a paper file","excerpt":"Why an eye clinic in India still runs on paper registers and carbon-copy prescriptions, and why we chose to build offline-first software for the machines clinics already own.","tags":["offline","emr","optometry","india"],"body":"There is a particular sound at nine in the morning in most optometry clinics we have sat in: the flip of a register, the scratch of a pen behind the front desk, the tear of a carbon sheet under a prescription pad. Paper is not a habit in these rooms, it is the system. It holds the refraction values, the follow-up dates, the balance at the optical counter, and it does all of this without asking anyone for a password.\n\nThe trouble shows up when a patient returns after two years and the file is on a shelf nobody can name, when a recall card was never written, or when a prescription has to be copied out by hand a second time for the optical shop. None of that is care. It is clerical weight on people who came to work in eye care.\n\nThe software meant to fix this usually assumes two things: a steady connection and a budget for machines to match. In the clinics we know, the connection drops and the budget is one laptop the owner already bought for billing. Any system that needs the network to open a patient's history is a system that fails exactly when the clinic is busiest.\n\nSo we decided to build FocusCaseX, an offline-first EMR workspace for eye care, made by us at CaseX Labs for Indian clinics. The rules are plain. A patient's page should be a file you can open, read and print, not a form buried in menus. The network should be optional, never a requirement. The machine it runs on should be the one already on the desk.\n\nThis log is where we will write about what we build and what clinics tell us, in the order it happens. The prototype is young and the list of what it cannot do yet is long. But the first patient pages already read like the files they are meant to replace, and that felt like the right place to start."}]};
